/* Essay Writing Workshop content (Student's Version). Kester Small. */
window.COURSE_WORKSHOP = {"chapters":[{"id":"process","n":1,"icon":"🧱","title":"The Writing Process","kicker":"From blank page to final draft","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W01.jpg","w":1000,"h":667,"alt":"A builder spreading mortar on a wall of concrete blocks.","by":"Solømen","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-person-is-putting-cement-on-a-brick-wall-i6V6diaf71A","cap":"A wall goes up one course of blocks at a time, each laid on the one below. An essay is built the same way: prewriting supports the draft, and the draft supports revision."},"objectives":["Name the five stages of the writing process and what happens in each.","Explain why skipping prewriting and revision costs marks under exam conditions.","Plan your time for a 50-minute Paper 02 essay."],"html":"<p><b>Writing is a process, not a single event.</b> Strong writers do not produce a perfect essay in one attempt. They move through five stages, and they often loop back: a new idea while drafting sends them back to planning; a weak paragraph found while revising sends them back to research.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What you do</th><th>Exam time (50-minute essay)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>1. Prewrite</b></td><td>Unpack the question, generate ideas, gather evidence, write a thesis and an outline.</td><td>8–10 minutes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Draft</b></td><td>Write the introduction, body paragraphs and conclusion from your outline. Keep moving; do not stop to perfect every sentence.</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Revise</b></td><td>Improve the <i>ideas and organisation</i>: is every paragraph on the thesis? Is anything missing, repeated or in the wrong place?</td><td>4 minutes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Edit</b></td><td>Correct the <i>surface</i>: grammar, spelling, punctuation, capital letters, word count.</td><td>4 minutes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Publish</b></td><td>Produce the final, clean copy. In the exam, this is your neat script with any crossings-out ruled through once.</td><td>—</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Revising is not the same as editing.</b> Revising changes <i>what</i> you say and <i>where</i> you say it (content and organisation). Editing changes <i>how it is written</i> (expression). The mark scheme rewards both: Content and Organization together are worth 17 of the 25 marks, Expression 8.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Candidates who start writing immediately often run out of points by the third paragraph or drift away from the question. Ten minutes of planning is not lost time; it is the time that earns the Content and Organization marks.</p>\n<h4>Unpack the question before anything else</h4>\n<p>Every question contains three things you must find before you plan: the <b>command word</b> (what to do), the <b>key terms</b> (what to write about) and the <b>limits</b> (how many points, which genre, which audience, how many words).</p>\n<div class=\"passage\"><p><i>Example:</i> \"In an essay of NO MORE than 300 words, <u>explain</u> <u>THREE</u> <u>causes</u> of <u>traffic congestion</u> in a Caribbean capital of your choice.\"</p><p>Command word: <b>explain</b> (give reasons and show how, not just list). Key terms: <b>causes</b> (not effects or solutions), <b>traffic congestion</b>, <b>a Caribbean capital</b>. Limits: <b>three</b> causes, <b>300 words</b>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Command word</th><th>What it asks you to do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Explain</td><td>Give reasons and show how or why something happens.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Discuss</td><td>Explore an issue from more than one angle, with developed points.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Describe</td><td>Give a clear, detailed account of features or events.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Evaluate / Assess</td><td>Judge the strengths and weaknesses or the worth of something and reach a conclusion.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compare / Contrast</td><td>Show similarities / show differences, point by point.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Argue / Persuade</td><td>Take a position and defend it / move an audience to believe or act.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"Please Don't Rush Every Paper","word":"PDREP","letters":[["P","Prewrite","Unpack, brainstorm, research, thesis, outline."],["D","Draft","Write it through from your outline."],["R","Revise","Fix ideas and order: add, remove, move, substitute."],["E","Edit","Fix the surface: capitals, usage, punctuation, spelling."],["P","Publish","Hand in a clean, final copy."]],"extra":"And unpack every question with <b>BUG</b>: <b>B</b>ox the command word, <b>U</b>nderline the key terms, <b>G</b>lance back at the question after you plan and again before you finish."},"check":[{"q":"A student moves her second body paragraph to the end because it is her strongest point. Which stage is she in?","o":["Editing","Revising","Publishing","Drafting"],"a":1,"why":"Changing the order of ideas is a change to organisation, which is revising. Editing deals with surface accuracy such as spelling and punctuation."},{"q":"In the question \"Discuss TWO ways the media shape Caribbean youth identity\", which word is the command word?","o":["Media","TWO","Discuss","Identity"],"a":2,"why":"\"Discuss\" tells you what to do: explore each way with developed points. \"TWO\" is a limit and \"media\" and \"identity\" are key terms."},{"q":"About how long should you spend planning a Paper 02 essay?","o":["None: start writing at once","2 minutes","8–10 minutes","25 minutes"],"a":2,"why":"With roughly 50 minutes per essay, 8–10 minutes of planning leaves about 30 minutes to draft and 8 to revise and edit."},{"q":"Which marks are earned mainly at the revising stage?","o":["Content and Organization","Expression only","Spelling only","None: revising does not earn marks"],"a":0,"why":"Revising strengthens what you say and how it is ordered, the Content (10) and Organization (7) marks."}],"task":{"prompt":"Use BUG on this question, then write down the command word, the key terms and the limits: <i>\"In an essay of NO MORE than 300 words, explain THREE ways in which young people in the Caribbean can reduce plastic waste at school.\"</i> Finish with one sentence saying what the essay must NOT do.","min":40,"max":120,"checklist":["I named the command word and what it requires.","I listed every key term.","I noted the number of points, the genre and the word limit.","I stated what would be off the question."]}},{"id":"prewrite","n":2,"icon":"💡","title":"Prewriting Strategies","kicker":"Generate ideas before you write","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W02.jpg","w":1000,"h":751,"alt":"A man looking at a blue wall covered in yellow sticky notes.","by":"Per Lööv","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/person-looking-at-sticky-note-wall-oXqn2CXC_9Q","cap":"Ideas on sticky notes can be grouped, moved and discarded: prewriting gets every possible idea out of your head so you can choose the strongest three."},"objectives":["Use five prewriting strategies: listing, freewriting, clustering, questioning and outlining.","Choose the right strategy for the genre and the time available.","Turn a cluster or list into a working outline."],"html":"<p><b>Prewriting</b> is everything you do before the first sentence of the draft. Its job is to give you <i>more ideas than you need</i>, so that you can choose the three strongest instead of writing the first three you think of.</p>\n<div class=\"ww-grid\">\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>1. Brainstorming (listing)</h4><p>Write every idea on the topic as a quick list, without judging any of them. Then circle the best and group the related ones.</p><p class=\"hint\">Best for: expository and argumentative essays under time pressure.</p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>2. Freewriting</h4><p>Write non-stop for three to five minutes on the topic, ignoring spelling and order. Underline any phrase worth keeping.</p><p class=\"hint\">Best for: narratives and reflective pieces, or when you feel stuck.</p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>3. Clustering (mind mapping)</h4><p>Put the topic in a central circle. Branch out to main ideas, then out again to examples and details. Each main branch can become a body paragraph.</p><p class=\"hint\">Best for: seeing how ideas connect; visual learners.</p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>4. Questioning (5Ws and H)</h4><p>Ask <b>Who? What? When? Where? Why? How?</b> about the topic. Journalists use this to make sure nothing important is missing.</p><p class=\"hint\">Best for: narratives (setting and plot) and explaining a process.</p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>5. Outlining</h4><p>Arrange your chosen ideas in order: thesis, then each body paragraph's point and evidence, then the conclusion. A <b>scratch outline</b> of short phrases is enough in an exam.</p><p class=\"hint\">Best for: every essay; it is the bridge from ideas to draft.</p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>Also useful: the T-chart</h4><p>Two columns, <b>For</b> and <b>Against</b> (or <b>Causes</b> and <b>Effects</b>). Essential before an argumentative essay, where you must answer the other side.</p><p class=\"hint\">Best for: argumentative and persuasive essays.</p></div>\n</div>\n<h4>From cluster to outline: a worked example</h4>\n<p><i>Topic: why young people in Sint Maarten should learn to swim.</i></p>\n<div class=\"ww-cluster\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A cluster diagram with the topic in the centre and three branches: safety, health and opportunity, each with two details.\"><div class=\"c0\">Learning to swim</div><div class=\"cb\"><b>Safety</b><span>island surrounded by sea</span><span>rip currents at some beaches</span></div><div class=\"cb\"><b>Health</b><span>full-body exercise</span><span>low cost: the sea is free</span></div><div class=\"cb\"><b>Opportunity</b><span>jobs in marine tourism</span><span>lifeguard and diving careers</span></div></div>\n<div class=\"passage\"><p><b>Scratch outline</b></p><p>Thesis: Every young person on an island should learn to swim because it saves lives, builds health and opens careers.<br>¶1 Safety: surrounded by sea; rip currents → swimmers can survive and help others.<br>¶2 Health: whole-body exercise, free in the sea → fights inactivity.<br>¶3 Opportunity: marine tourism, lifeguard, dive instructor → island economy.<br>Conclusion: restate; call for swimming in the PE programme.</p></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Your plan can be done in the answer booklet's planning space. Rule a single line through it when you finish so the examiner knows it is not part of the essay.</p>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"Big Fish Can Quickly Outswim","word":"BFCQO","letters":[["B","Brainstorm","List every idea fast; judge later."],["F","Freewrite","Write non-stop for 3–5 minutes."],["C","Cluster","Map ideas in branches around the topic."],["Q","Question","Who? What? When? Where? Why? How?"],["O","Outline","Order your best ideas: thesis, points, conclusion."]],"extra":"The big fish (your strongest ideas) only get out of the net if you cast it wide first."},"check":[{"q":"Which strategy asks Who? What? When? Where? Why? How?","o":["Freewriting","Clustering","Questioning","Outlining"],"a":2,"why":"Questioning uses the journalist's 5Ws and H to make sure no important detail is missing."},{"q":"You are writing an argumentative essay and must answer the opposing view. Which tool helps most?","o":["A T-chart of For and Against","Freewriting about your feelings","A 5Ws list","A spelling checklist"],"a":0,"why":"A T-chart lays out both sides, so you can choose your strongest reasons and the counter-argument you will rebut."},{"q":"In a cluster diagram, the main branches usually become…","o":["the hook","the body paragraphs","the title","the conclusion"],"a":1,"why":"Each main branch is a separate idea with its own details: exactly what a body paragraph needs."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of prewriting?","o":["To write the introduction","To check spelling","To produce more ideas than you need so you can choose the best","To count the words"],"a":2,"why":"Prewriting widens your options so the essay is built from your strongest ideas, not just your first ones."}],"task":{"prompt":"Choose ONE topic: (a) the effects of tourism on your island, (b) a day the power went out, (c) whether school uniforms should be abolished. Brainstorm at least eight ideas, group them into three branches, then write a scratch outline: a thesis and one line for each body paragraph.","min":60,"max":200,"checklist":["I listed at least eight ideas.","I grouped them into three branches.","I wrote a one-sentence thesis.","Each body paragraph has a point and a detail."]}},{"id":"research","n":3,"icon":"🔎","title":"Research and Evidence","kicker":"Finding, testing and recording support","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W03.jpg","w":1000,"h":667,"alt":"A young woman working on a laptop at a wooden table in front of bookshelves.","by":"Annie Spratt","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/woman-in-black-jacket-using-macbook-pro-xKJUnFwfz3s","cap":"Research blends books, websites and people. The skill is not just finding information but judging whether it deserves a place in your essay."},"objectives":["Distinguish primary from secondary sources and choose the right one.","Test any source with the CRAAP test.","Take notes by quoting, paraphrasing and summarising, and credit every source."],"html":"<p><b>Evidence</b> is what turns an opinion into a convincing point. In Paper 02 your evidence comes mostly from the extract or stimulus in front of you and from your own knowledge. In Paper 032 and in class essays you research the topic in advance, so you must know how to find good sources and use them honestly.</p>\n<h4>Primary and secondary sources</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Primary</th><th>Secondary</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>What it is</b></td><td>First-hand information you collect or that comes directly from the event.</td><td>Information that someone else has collected, interpreted or summarised.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Examples</b></td><td>Your own interview or questionnaire, an eyewitness account, a speech, raw statistics from a government department.</td><td>A newspaper article about a survey, a textbook chapter, an encyclopedia entry, a documentary.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Strength</b></td><td>Original and specific to your question.</td><td>Quick to find; gives background and context.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Five kinds of evidence</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Facts and statistics:</b> figures from a credible body such as a national statistics department, the Caribbean Public Health Agency (CARPHA) or a ministry report.</li>\n<li><b>Examples:</b> a specific, named case (\"the closing of Front Street to traffic on cruise days\").</li>\n<li><b>Expert opinion:</b> the view of someone qualified, named with their role.</li>\n<li><b>Anecdote:</b> a short real experience; powerful in narratives and persuasive pieces, weak as the only evidence in an argument.</li>\n<li><b>Textual evidence:</b> a short, exact quotation from the extract, the most important kind in Module 1 and Module 2 essays.</li></ul>\n<h4>Test every source: CRAAP</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>C</b>urrency</td><td>When was it published or updated? Is it recent enough for the topic?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>R</b>elevance</td><td>Does it answer <i>your</i> question for <i>your</i> audience?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>A</b>uthority</td><td>Who wrote it? What qualifies them? Is the organisation reputable?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>A</b>ccuracy</td><td>Is it supported by evidence? Can you confirm it in a second, independent source?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>P</b>urpose</td><td>Why does it exist: to inform, to sell, to persuade? Is it biased?</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>CRAAP is the same thinking you use in Module 1 when you evaluate the <b>credibility</b> of an extract: <i>credibility and authority, currency and relevance, reliability</i>.</p>\n<h4>Taking notes you can use</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Quote</b> when the exact words matter. Keep quotations short and copy them exactly, inside quotation marks.</li>\n<li><b>Paraphrase</b> when you need the detail but not the wording: rewrite the idea fully in your own words and sentence structure.</li>\n<li><b>Summarise</b> when you need only the main point: reduce a passage to one or two sentences.</li>\n<li>Record the <b>author, title, publisher or website and date</b> next to every note, so you can credit it.</li></ul>\n<p>When you use a quotation in an essay, <b>ICE</b> it: <b>I</b>ntroduce it (who said it and in what context), <b>C</b>ite it (the short quotation itself), <b>E</b>xplain it (what it shows and why it matters to your point).</p>\n<p class=\"warn\"><b>Plagiarism</b> is presenting someone else's words or ideas as your own. Changing a few words is still plagiarism. Credit the source, or genuinely rewrite the idea and still credit it.</p>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"CRAAP test","word":"CRAAP","letters":[["C","Currency","Is it recent enough?"],["R","Relevance","Does it fit my question and audience?"],["A","Authority","Who wrote it, and are they qualified?"],["A","Accuracy","Is it supported and confirmed elsewhere?"],["P","Purpose","Why was it written? Is it biased?"]],"extra":"Then <b>ICE</b> every quotation: <b>I</b>ntroduce, <b>C</b>ite, <b>E</b>xplain."},"check":[{"q":"You interview ten Form 5 students about their study habits. This is…","o":["a secondary source","a primary source","expert opinion","plagiarism"],"a":1,"why":"You collected the information first-hand, so it is primary data."},{"q":"A website about a skin cream is written by the company that sells the cream. Which CRAAP letter raises the biggest concern?","o":["Currency","Relevance","Purpose","None"],"a":2,"why":"The purpose is to sell, so the information may be biased."},{"q":"Which is a genuine paraphrase of \"Regular reading improves vocabulary and concentration\"?","o":["Regular reading improves vocabulary and focus.","Reading often helps you learn more words and stay focused for longer.","\"Regular reading improves vocabulary.\"","Reading is good."],"a":1,"why":"It keeps the full meaning but changes both the words and the sentence structure. Option A swaps one word only, which is too close to the original."},{"q":"In ICE, what does the E stand for?","o":["Evidence","Explain","Example","Evaluate"],"a":1,"why":"After introducing and citing a quotation, you explain what it shows and how it supports your point."}],"task":{"prompt":"Find or recall TWO sources on a topic of your choice. For each, write the full reference (author or organisation, title, date) and test it with CRAAP in one line per letter. Then write one sentence that ICEs a short quotation from the stronger source.","min":80,"max":220,"checklist":["I gave a full reference for each source.","I tested both sources with all five CRAAP letters.","I said which source is stronger and why.","My quotation is introduced, cited and explained."]}},{"id":"thesis","n":4,"icon":"🧭","title":"Writing a Strong Thesis Statement","kicker":"One sentence that steers the whole essay","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W04.jpg","w":1000,"h":674,"alt":"A hand holding a compass up in front of a forest valley.","by":"Jamie Street","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/person-holding-compass-facing-towards-green-pine-trees-_94HLr_QXo8","cap":"A thesis works like a compass: every paragraph should point in the direction it sets, and the reader always knows where the essay is heading."},"objectives":["Define a thesis statement and say where it goes.","Write a thesis with the TAP formula for each genre.","Diagnose and repair weak theses."],"html":"<p>A <b>thesis statement</b> is the one sentence, usually the last sentence of the introduction, that states the main idea of the whole essay and previews how it will be developed. Every body paragraph must support it. If a paragraph does not, either the paragraph or the thesis needs to change.</p>\n<h4>The TAP formula</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-formula\"><span><b>T</b>opic</span><i>+</i><span><b>A</b>ngle<br><small>your position or controlling idea</small></span><i>+</i><span><b>P</b>oints<br><small>the reasons or parts you will develop</small></span></div>\n<p><i>Topic:</i> traffic congestion in Philipsburg. <i>Angle:</i> it has three main causes. <i>Points:</i> narrow historic streets, rising car ownership and cruise-day crowds.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Thesis:</b> Traffic congestion in Philipsburg is caused mainly by its narrow historic streets, the steady rise in private car ownership and the crowds that arrive on cruise-ship days.</p>\n<h4>A thesis for every genre</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Genre</th><th>The thesis…</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Expository</b></td><td>States what will be explained and previews the parts. No opinion to defend.</td><td>Sargassum harms Caribbean coastlines in three ways: it damages tourism, threatens fisheries and releases harmful gases as it rots.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Argumentative</b></td><td>Takes a clear, debatable position and previews the reasons.</td><td>Secondary schools should allow phones in class for guided learning, because phones give instant access to research, build digital responsibility and cost schools nothing.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Persuasive</b></td><td>Takes a position aimed at a specific audience and points towards action.</td><td>Every student at our school should give one Saturday morning to the Great Bay clean-up, because our beach, our health and our future depend on it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Narrative</b></td><td>Often implied rather than stated: a <b>controlling insight</b> the story reveals, sometimes hinted at in the opening or stated at the end.</td><td>That night I learned that a calm voice can be stronger shelter than any roof.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Test your thesis: is it SOS?</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Specific:</b> it names the exact topic and points, not \"many things\" or \"various ways\".</li>\n<li><b>One sentence:</b> it is a single, complete statement, not a question or a fragment.</li>\n<li><b>Supportable:</b> each point can be developed with evidence in one paragraph.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Weak</b>In this essay I will talk about social media.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Strong</b>Social media has changed how Caribbean teenagers communicate by speeding up news, blending Creole and Standard English online and replacing many face-to-face conversations.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Weak</b>Is school uniform a good thing?</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Strong</b>School uniforms should remain compulsory because they reduce the pressure to compete on fashion, build a sense of belonging and keep students identifiable on trips.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Weak</b>Pollution is bad and there are many causes and effects and solutions.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Strong</b>Plastic pollution on Caribbean beaches comes mainly from single-use packaging, poor waste collection and litter washed in by the sea.</div></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Avoid announcing (\"In this essay I will…\") and avoid questions. Both waste words and give the examiner no position. Keep the thesis to about 25–40 words in a 300-word essay.</p>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"TAP your thesis","word":"TAP","letters":[["T","Topic","What exactly is the essay about?"],["A","Angle","What is your position or controlling idea?"],["P","Points","Which reasons or parts will you develop?"]],"extra":"Then check it is <b>SOS</b>: <b>S</b>pecific, <b>O</b>ne sentence, <b>S</b>upportable."},"check":[{"q":"Where does the thesis statement usually appear?","o":["In the title","At the end of the introduction","At the start of the conclusion","In the middle of the first body paragraph"],"a":1,"why":"The hook and background lead into the thesis, which closes the introduction and sets up the body."},{"q":"Which is the strongest thesis?","o":["In this essay I will discuss Carnival.","Carnival is a big festival.","Carnival communicates Caribbean identity through its music, its costumes and its use of public space.","What does Carnival mean to us?"],"a":2,"why":"It is specific, a single statement and previews three supportable points. The others announce, state a vague fact or ask a question."},{"q":"An expository thesis differs from an argumentative thesis because it…","o":["takes a debatable position","explains without arguing for a side","must be a question","never previews points"],"a":1,"why":"Expository writing explains and informs. Argumentative writing defends a position that someone could disagree with."},{"q":"In TAP, the A stands for…","o":["Audience","Angle","Argument","Anecdote"],"a":1,"why":"The angle is your position or controlling idea about the topic."}],"task":{"prompt":"Write a TAP thesis for each of these: (1) an <b>expository</b> essay on the causes of youth unemployment in the Caribbean; (2) an <b>argumentative</b> essay on whether homework should be banned; (3) a <b>persuasive</b> piece urging Form 4 students to join the school's reading club. Label T, A and P in one of them.","min":60,"max":160,"checklist":["Each thesis is one sentence.","Each thesis previews its points.","The argumentative and persuasive theses take a clear side.","I labelled T, A and P in one thesis."]}},{"id":"hooks","n":5,"icon":"🪝","title":"Hooks and Introductions","kicker":"Catch the reader in the first sentence","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W05.jpg","w":1000,"h":667,"alt":"A single fishing hook hanging against a pale blue sky.","by":"Kaptured by Kasia","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-fishing-hook-hanging-from-the-side-of-a-boat-Fl3Rf_t8dMs","cap":"A hook is the first sentence of your essay. Its only job is to catch the reader and pull them towards your thesis."},"objectives":["Write six kinds of hook and choose one that suits the genre and audience.","Build an introduction with the Hook–Bridge–Thesis structure.","Avoid the clichés examiners see most often."],"html":"<p>The <b>introduction</b> has three jobs: capture interest, give the reader enough context to follow you, and state your thesis. In a 300-word essay it should be about 40–60 words; in a 500-word essay, about 60–80.</p>\n<h4>Hook – Bridge – Thesis</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-hbt\"><div><b>Hook</b><span>One or two sentences that grab attention.</span></div><div><b>Bridge</b><span>One or two sentences of background that connect the hook to the topic.</span></div><div><b>Thesis</b><span>Your TAP statement, the last sentence of the paragraph.</span></div></div>\n<h4>Six kinds of hook: QUOTES</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Hook</th><th>Example</th><th>Works well for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Q</b>uestion</td><td>What would you do if the only road into town were blocked every afternoon?</td><td>Persuasive, argumentative</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>U</b>nusual fact or statistic</td><td>Saint Martin is one small island shared by two countries, and its roads carry the traffic of both.</td><td>Expository, argumentative</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>O</b>ne-line anecdote</td><td>My grandmother still keeps the transistor radio that told her Hurricane Luis was coming.</td><td>Narrative, persuasive</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>T</b>ruthful bold statement</td><td>The most dangerous thing on our beaches is not the sea; it is what we leave behind.</td><td>Persuasive, argumentative</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E</b>xpert quotation</td><td>\"Prevention is better than cure,\" health officials across the region repeat, yet…</td><td>Expository, argumentative</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>S</b>cene-setting description</td><td>The streetlights flickered, the rain thickened and the radio began to hiss.</td><td>Narrative</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Hooks to avoid</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Dictionary openings:</b> \"The Oxford Dictionary defines communication as…\" (examiners see this constantly).</li>\n<li><b>\"Since the beginning of time…\"</b> or other sweeping claims you cannot support.</li>\n<li><b>A question you never answer</b>, or a run of three questions in a row.</li>\n<li><b>A hook from a different topic</b> that the bridge cannot connect.</li>\n<li>In formal expository and argumentative essays, <b>slang or Creole in the narration</b> (Creole is welcome in a narrative's dialogue).</li></ul>\n<h4>A complete introduction</h4>\n<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag h\">Hook</span> On a busy weekday afternoon, the short drive from Philipsburg to Cole Bay can take many times longer than it should. <span class=\"ww-tag b\">Bridge</span> For a capital squeezed between the Great Salt Pond and the sea, every extra car competes for the same few streets. <span class=\"ww-tag t\">Thesis</span> Traffic congestion in Philipsburg is caused mainly by its narrow historic streets, the steady rise in private car ownership and the crowds that arrive on cruise-ship days.</p></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Write the thesis first in your plan, then choose a hook that leads to it. A brilliant hook that does not connect to the thesis loses Organization marks.</p>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"Hungry Birds Tweet · QUOTES","word":"HBT","letters":[["H","Hook","Grab attention in one or two sentences."],["B","Bridge","Give the background that connects hook to topic."],["T","Thesis","State your TAP sentence last."]],"extra":"Choose a hook from <b>QUOTES</b>: <b>Q</b>uestion · <b>U</b>nusual fact · <b>O</b>ne-line anecdote · <b>T</b>ruthful bold statement · <b>E</b>xpert quotation · <b>S</b>cene-setting description."},"check":[{"q":"Which hook best suits a narrative?","o":["An unusual statistic","A scene-setting description","A dictionary definition","A list of three questions"],"a":1,"why":"A narrative draws the reader into a time and place, so a vivid scene is the natural hook."},{"q":"What is the job of the bridge?","o":["To state the thesis","To connect the hook to the topic with background","To give the conclusion early","To add a counter-argument"],"a":1,"why":"The bridge supplies the context that links an attention-grabbing hook to the specific thesis."},{"q":"Why do examiners dislike dictionary openings?","o":["They are too short","They are overused and rarely add insight","They are always wrong","They are in Creole"],"a":1,"why":"They are predictable and usually tell the reader nothing the question has not already assumed."},{"q":"In QUOTES, the O stands for…","o":["Opinion","One-line anecdote","Overview","Opposing view"],"a":1,"why":"A short personal or real-life story told in a single line."}],"task":{"prompt":"Using this thesis, write THREE different hooks (choose from QUOTES and name each one), then write a full Hook–Bridge–Thesis introduction with your best hook: <i>\"Young people should learn a second Caribbean language, such as Spanish or Papiamentu, because it opens regional jobs, deepens friendships across islands and sharpens the mind.\"</i>","min":70,"max":180,"checklist":["I wrote three hooks of different types and named them.","My introduction has a hook, a bridge and the thesis.","The hook connects naturally to the thesis.","I avoided dictionary and \"since the beginning of time\" openings."]}},{"id":"body","n":6,"icon":"🪨","title":"Topic Sentences and Body Paragraphs","kicker":"One point, fully developed, in every paragraph","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W06.jpg","w":1000,"h":750,"alt":"Stepping stones crossing a calm river to stone steps on the far bank.","by":"Peter Campbell Smith","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-small-river-with-rocks-and-plants-nk4RnC_bjGk","cap":"Body paragraphs are stepping stones: each one must be solid on its own and placed so the reader can step easily to the next, all the way to the conclusion."},"objectives":["Write a topic sentence with a point and a controlling idea that links to the thesis.","Develop a paragraph with PEEL.","Adapt the paragraph for narrative writing."],"html":"<p>A <b>topic sentence</b> is the first sentence of a body paragraph. It states the paragraph's single main point and links it to the thesis. Readers, and examiners, often read topic sentences first to see whether the essay is organised.</p>\n<h4>Anatomy of a topic sentence</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-formula\"><span><b>Transition</b><br><small>where we are</small></span><i>+</i><span><b>Topic</b><br><small>which point from the thesis</small></span><i>+</i><span><b>Controlling idea</b><br><small>what you will say about it</small></span></div>\n<p class=\"passage\"><i>First</i>, <i>Philipsburg's narrow historic streets</i> <i>were laid out for carts and pedestrians, not for thousands of cars.</i></p>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Weak</b>In this paragraph I will talk about the streets.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Strong</b>First, Philipsburg's narrow historic streets were laid out for carts and pedestrians, not for thousands of cars.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Weak (a fact, not a point)</b>There are many cars in Sint Maarten.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Strong</b>Second, rising private car ownership puts more vehicles on the road than the network was ever designed to carry.</div></div>\n<h4>Build the paragraph with PEEL</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>P</b>oint</td><td>The topic sentence: one clear idea that supports the thesis.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E</b>vidence</td><td>A fact, example, quotation or detail that proves the point.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E</b>xplain</td><td>Show <i>how</i> and <i>why</i> the evidence proves the point. This is where most marks are won or lost.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>L</b>ink</td><td>Connect back to the thesis or forward to the next paragraph.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag p\">P</span> First, Philipsburg's narrow historic streets were laid out for carts and pedestrians, not for thousands of cars. <span class=\"ww-tag e\">E</span> Front Street and Back Street are narrow streets squeezed between rows of buildings, with almost no room to widen them. <span class=\"ww-tag x\">E</span> When a single delivery truck stops to unload, the whole lane stops behind it, and because there are few alternative routes, the delay spreads quickly to the roads leading into town. <span class=\"ww-tag l\">L</span> The town's layout therefore creates a bottleneck before a single extra car arrives.</p></div>\n<h4>How many words?</h4>\n<p>In a 300-word essay: introduction about 50, three body paragraphs of about 65–70, conclusion about 40. In a 500-word essay: introduction about 70, three body paragraphs of about 110, conclusion about 60.</p>\n<h4>Narrative paragraphs work differently</h4>\n<p>A story is not built from PEEL paragraphs. Each paragraph covers <b>one moment, place or speaker</b>, and you start a new paragraph when the time, place or speaker changes. Every new speaker in dialogue gets a new line. Instead of explaining, you <b>show</b>: action, dialogue and sensory detail carry the meaning.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> The Explain step is the one weaker candidates skip. After every piece of evidence, ask \"So what?\" and answer it in a full sentence.</p>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"PEEL the paragraph","word":"PEEL","letters":[["P","Point","One clear idea in the topic sentence."],["E","Evidence","Fact, example, detail or short quotation."],["E","Explain","How and why does it prove the point? Ask \"So what?\""],["L","Link","Back to the thesis or on to the next point."]],"extra":"Topic sentence = <b>Transition + Topic + Controlling idea</b>."},"check":[{"q":"Which is the best topic sentence for a paragraph on the health benefits of swimming?","o":["Swimming.","In this paragraph I will discuss health.","Swimming also builds health, because it exercises the whole body without straining the joints.","Many people swim."],"a":2,"why":"It has a transition (also), a topic (swimming and health) and a controlling idea (whole-body exercise without strain)."},{"q":"Which PEEL step answers the question \"So what?\"","o":["Point","Evidence","Explain","Link"],"a":2,"why":"Explaining shows how the evidence proves the point, which is the analysis examiners reward."},{"q":"When should a narrative start a new paragraph?","o":["Every 50 words","When the time, place or speaker changes","Only at the climax","Never"],"a":1,"why":"Narrative paragraphs follow moments, places and speakers, and each new speaker in dialogue begins a new line."},{"q":"About how many words should each of three body paragraphs be in a 300-word essay?","o":["20","65–70","150","200"],"a":1,"why":"Three paragraphs of about 65–70 words leave room for a 50-word introduction and a 40-word conclusion."}],"task":{"prompt":"Write ONE PEEL body paragraph (about 70 words) for this thesis: <i>\"Every young person on an island should learn to swim because it saves lives, builds health and opens careers.\"</i> Label P, E, E and L in brackets.","min":55,"max":110,"checklist":["My topic sentence links to the thesis.","I gave specific evidence.","I explained how the evidence proves the point.","I linked back or forward.","I labelled each PEEL step."]}},{"id":"transitions","n":7,"icon":"🌉","title":"Transitions and Coherence","kicker":"Bridges between ideas","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W07.jpg","w":1000,"h":750,"alt":"A wooden footbridge crossing a small stream in a green forest.","by":"Grant Durr","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-wooden-bridge-over-a-small-stream-in-a-forest-JlIWEgEVeGE","cap":"Without a bridge, the walker has to jump. Without transitions, the reader has to guess how one idea connects to the next."},"objectives":["Choose transitions by their function, not at random.","Link sentences within a paragraph using the old–new chain.","Avoid overused and misused connectives."],"html":"<p><b>Coherence</b> means the essay hangs together: every sentence follows logically from the one before. <b>Transitions</b> (also called connectives or signposts) are the words and phrases that show the reader <i>how</i> two ideas are related. Using the right one earns Organization marks; using the wrong one confuses the meaning.</p>\n<h4>Choose by function: ACCESS</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Transitions</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>A</b>ddition</td><td>Furthermore, In addition, Moreover, Also, Similarly</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>C</b>ontrast</td><td>However, On the other hand, Nevertheless, In contrast, Although, Whereas</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>C</b>ause and effect</td><td>Therefore, As a result, Consequently, Because of this, This leads to</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E</b>xample</td><td>For example, For instance, Such as, In particular, This is seen in</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>S</b>equence</td><td>First, Second, Next, Then, Meanwhile, Later, Finally</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>S</b>ummary</td><td>In conclusion, Overall, Ultimately, In short, Clearly</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>The old–new chain</h4>\n<p>Strong paragraphs link sentences by starting with something <b>old</b> (already mentioned) and ending with something <b>new</b>. The new idea at the end of one sentence becomes the old idea at the start of the next.</p>\n<div class=\"passage\"><p>Cruise ships bring thousands of <u>visitors</u> into Philipsburg on a single morning. <u>These visitors</u> need <u>taxis, tour buses and shuttles</u>. <u>All of these vehicles</u> converge on the same few streets around the harbour.</p></div>\n<h4>Transitions in a narrative</h4>\n<p>Stories move through <b>time</b>, so they rely on time transitions: <i>That evening, Moments later, By midnight, The next morning, At last</i>. Vary them; a story where every paragraph starts with \"Then\" feels flat.</p>\n<h4>Common mistakes</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Wrong function:</b> \"The beach is clean. <s>Therefore</s> the sea is warm.\" (no cause and effect). Use <i>Also</i>, or no transition.</li>\n<li><b>\"However\" as a joiner:</b> \"Cars are expensive<s>, however</s> many families buy two.\" Write: \"Cars are expensive; however, many families buy two.\" or \"Although cars are expensive, many families buy two.\"</li>\n<li><b>Overuse:</b> a transition at the start of every sentence becomes noise. Use them at paragraph openings and at real turns in the logic.</li>\n<li><b>Informal connectives</b> in formal essays: \"Plus\", \"Also too\", \"So yeah\".</li></ul>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"ACCESS your transitions","word":"ACCESS","letters":[["A","Addition","Furthermore, Moreover, In addition"],["C","Contrast","However, In contrast, Nevertheless"],["C","Cause and effect","Therefore, As a result, Consequently"],["E","Example","For example, For instance, Such as"],["S","Sequence","First, Next, Then, Finally"],["S","Summary","In conclusion, Overall, Ultimately"]],"extra":"Ask: <i>how</i> is this idea related to the last one? Then pick the transition from that row."},"check":[{"q":"Which transition fits? \"Fuel prices rose sharply. ___, more people began car-pooling.\"","o":["However","For example","As a result","Similarly"],"a":2,"why":"The second sentence is a consequence of the first, so a cause-and-effect transition fits."},{"q":"Which sentence uses \"however\" correctly?","o":["I studied, however I failed.","I studied; however, I failed.","However I studied I failed.","I studied however, I failed."],"a":1,"why":"\"However\" joins two complete sentences only with a semicolon before it and a comma after it."},{"q":"The old–new chain means each sentence…","o":["starts with a transition","begins with something already mentioned and ends with something new","repeats the thesis","is shorter than the last"],"a":1,"why":"Picking up the previous sentence's new idea makes the paragraph flow without relying on connectives."},{"q":"Which function does \"In contrast\" serve?","o":["Addition","Contrast","Sequence","Summary"],"a":1,"why":"It signals that the next idea differs from or opposes the previous one."}],"task":{"prompt":"Rewrite this paragraph, adding at least FOUR transitions from different ACCESS rows and using the old–new chain at least once: <i>\"Many students skip breakfast. They feel tired by second period. Their marks can suffer. Some schools offer a breakfast programme. Attendance at those schools has improved. Every school should consider one.\"</i>","min":45,"max":120,"checklist":["I used at least four transitions.","They come from at least three different functions.","Each transition matches the logical relationship.","I used the old–new chain at least once."]}},{"id":"conclusions","n":8,"icon":"🌅","title":"Conclusions That Close Strongly","kicker":"Leave the reader with something to keep","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W08.jpg","w":1000,"h":750,"alt":"A sunset over a rocky shoreline, with a ship on the horizon.","by":"Mitchel Wijt","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-beach-with-rocks-and-water-QVmzAT1kgEg","cap":"A good conclusion is like a sunset: it signals clearly that the day is ending and leaves a final image the reader remembers."},"objectives":["Write a conclusion with the 3 Rs: Restate, Recap, Resonate.","Match the ending to the genre.","Avoid new points and weak endings."],"html":"<p>The <b>conclusion</b> tells the examiner that your argument or explanation is complete. It should be shorter than a body paragraph (about 35–45 words in a 300-word essay) and must <b>not introduce a new point</b>.</p>\n<h4>The 3 Rs</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-hbt\"><div><b>Restate</b><span>Return to the thesis in fresh words. Do not copy it.</span></div><div><b>Recap</b><span>Briefly gather the main points, ideally in one sentence.</span></div><div><b>Resonate</b><span>End with significance: a wider implication, a recommendation, a call to action or a final image.</span></div></div>\n<h4>Endings by genre</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Genre</th><th>Resonate with…</th><th>Example final sentence</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Expository</b></td><td>The significance of understanding the topic, or a sensible next step.</td><td>Understanding these causes is the first step towards a capital where a short drive takes a short time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Argumentative</b></td><td>A firm, reasoned final judgement.</td><td>Used wisely, the phone in a student's pocket is not a distraction but the most powerful textbook the school will never have to buy.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Persuasive</b></td><td>A direct, specific call to action.</td><td>Bring a hat, bring a friend and meet us at the boardwalk at seven on Saturday.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Narrative</b></td><td>A resolution and a moment of reflection or change (the insight).</td><td>The roof leaked until dawn, but I have never felt safer than I did in the sound of her voice.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Endings to avoid</h4>\n<ul><li>\"In conclusion, as I have said…\" followed by the thesis word for word.</li>\n<li>A brand-new point that was never developed (\"Another cause is…\").</li>\n<li>An apology or a shrug (\"This is just my opinion\").</li>\n<li>In a narrative: \"And then I woke up and it was all a dream.\"</li></ul>\n<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag t\">Restate</span> Philipsburg's traffic is not simply bad luck. <span class=\"ww-tag b\">Recap</span> Old streets, more cars and cruise-day crowds combine to fill a small capital beyond its capacity. <span class=\"ww-tag h\">Resonate</span> Understanding these causes is the first step towards a capital where a short drive takes a short time.</p></div>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"The 3 Rs","word":"RRR","letters":[["R","Restate","The thesis, in fresh words."],["R","Recap","The main points, in one sentence."],["R","Resonate","Why it matters: implication, action or final image."]],"extra":"Golden rule: <b>no new points</b> in the conclusion."},"check":[{"q":"Which belongs in a conclusion?","o":["A new piece of evidence","A restatement of the thesis in fresh words","A new body point","The hook from the introduction copied exactly"],"a":1,"why":"Restating (not copying) the thesis signals closure without adding unsupported material."},{"q":"A persuasive piece should usually resonate with…","o":["a call to action","a dictionary definition","a new statistic","an apology"],"a":0,"why":"Persuasion aims to move the audience to act, so it ends by telling them what to do."},{"q":"How long should the conclusion of a 300-word essay be?","o":["About 10 words","About 35–45 words","About 100 words","Half the essay"],"a":1,"why":"Shorter than a body paragraph, but long enough to restate, recap and resonate."},{"q":"What is wrong with \"Another reason is cost, which is very important\" in a conclusion?","o":["It is too short","It introduces a new point","It uses a transition","Nothing"],"a":1,"why":"A new point in the conclusion cannot be developed and weakens organisation."}],"task":{"prompt":"Write a 3R conclusion (35–45 words) for this thesis: <i>\"Every young person on an island should learn to swim because it saves lives, builds health and opens careers.\"</i> Then rewrite only the last sentence so it ends as a <b>persuasive</b> call to action.","min":40,"max":90,"checklist":["I restated the thesis in fresh words.","I recapped all three points.","My final sentence resonates.","I added no new point.","My persuasive version ends with a clear action."]}},{"id":"revise","n":9,"icon":"🖍️","title":"Revising and Editing","kicker":"Turn a draft into a Grade One script","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W09.jpg","w":1000,"h":667,"alt":"A hand holding a red pen above a blank spiral notebook on a wooden desk.","by":"Kelly Sikkema","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/person-holding-red-pencil-writing-on-white-paper-Plso5cHu9w0","cap":"The red pen is the writer's best friend. Every strong essay you have read was improved by someone willing to cut, move and correct."},"objectives":["Revise for content and organisation with ARMS.","Edit for accuracy with CUPS.","Fix the Standard English errors examiners report most often."],"html":"<p>Revising and editing are two different passes. Do the <b>revising</b> pass first: there is no point correcting the spelling of a sentence you are about to delete.</p>\n<div class=\"ww-grid two\"><div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>Revise with ARMS</h4><ul><li><b>A</b>dd: missing evidence, an explanation, a transition, a counter-argument.</li><li><b>R</b>emove: repetition, off-topic sentences, filler (\"basically\", \"in today's society\").</li><li><b>M</b>ove: put points in the thesis order, strongest point where it belongs.</li><li><b>S</b>ubstitute: a vague word for a precise one, a weak example for a stronger one.</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>Edit with CUPS</h4><ul><li><b>C</b>apitalisation: proper nouns (Caribbean, Philipsburg, CARICOM), sentence starts, titles.</li><li><b>U</b>sage: subject–verb agreement, tense consistency, word choice (their/there, affect/effect).</li><li><b>P</b>unctuation: full stops, commas, apostrophes, no comma splices.</li><li><b>S</b>pelling: especially subject words: <i>communication, environment, government, necessary, receive</i>.</li></ul></div></div>\n<h4>Five errors examiners report most often</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Wrong</th><th>Right</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Comma splice</td><td>Tourism brings jobs, it also brings litter.</td><td>Tourism brings jobs; it also brings litter. / Tourism brings jobs, but it also brings litter.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subject–verb agreement</td><td>The number of cars are rising.</td><td>The number of cars is rising.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tense shift</td><td>She opened the door and sees the flood.</td><td>She opened the door and saw the flood.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apostrophes</td><td>The schools policy · it's message</td><td>The school's policy · its message</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fragment</td><td>Because the roads are narrow.</td><td>Traffic builds quickly because the roads are narrow.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Before and after</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Draft</b>Basically in todays society alot of people uses there phones to much, it effects them alot and also they dont talk face to face no more.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Revised and edited</b>Many people now spend so much time on their phones that face-to-face conversation is declining.</div></div>\n<p>ARMS removed the filler (\"basically\", \"in today's society\") and the vague \"effects them a lot\". CUPS fixed <i>today's, a lot, use, their, too, affects, don't</i> and the Creole double negative in a formal essay, and the comma splice disappeared.</p>\n<h4>Check the word count</h4>\n<p>Count your words before you finish: examiners stop reading at the limit, so anything beyond it earns nothing. A quick method is to count the words in three typical lines, find the average per line and multiply by the number of lines.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Read your essay \"in your head aloud\", slowly. Your ear catches missing words and tense shifts that your eye skims over.</p>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"Strong ARMS, clean CUPS","word":"ARMS + CUPS","letters":[["A","Add","What is missing?"],["R","Remove","What repeats or wanders off topic?"],["M","Move","Is everything in the best order?"],["S","Substitute","Which words could be more precise?"],["C","Capitalisation","Names, places, sentence starts."],["U","Usage","Agreement, tense, word choice."],["P","Punctuation","Full stops, commas, apostrophes."],["S","Spelling","Check the subject words."]],"extra":"Revise (ARMS) first, then edit (CUPS)."},"check":[{"q":"Moving your strongest point to match the thesis order is part of…","o":["CUPS","ARMS","QUOTES","TAP"],"a":1,"why":"Moving content to improve organisation is the M in ARMS, part of revising."},{"q":"Which sentence is correct?","o":["The number of tourists are growing.","The number of tourists is growing.","The number of tourists be growing.","The number of tourist are growing."],"a":1,"why":"The subject is \"the number\" (singular), so the verb is \"is\"."},{"q":"Which is a comma splice?","o":["Although it rained, we went.","It rained; we went anyway.","It rained, we went anyway.","It rained, but we went anyway."],"a":2,"why":"Two complete sentences joined only by a comma form a comma splice."},{"q":"Why revise before editing?","o":["Editing is optional","You may delete sentences, so correcting them first wastes time","Examiners only mark revising","It is the same thing"],"a":1,"why":"Fix the big things (ideas and order) first, then polish the sentences that survive."}],"task":{"prompt":"Revise and edit this paragraph using ARMS and CUPS, then list three changes you made and why: <i>\"In conclusion their is alot of reason why student should read more, it help them in there exam and also it is fun. basically reading is very very good and every body should do it because of the fact that it is good.\"</i>","min":40,"max":140,"checklist":["I removed filler and repetition.","I fixed every spelling and capitalisation error.","I fixed the comma splice and agreement errors.","I explained three of my changes."]}},{"id":"expository","n":10,"icon":"📘","title":"The Expository Essay","genre":true,"kicker":"Explain, inform, clarify","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W10.jpg","w":1000,"h":667,"alt":"A magnifying glass lying on a pale blue surface.","by":"Markus Winkler","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/magnifying-glass-on-white-table-afW1hht0NSs","cap":"Expository writing puts a topic under the magnifying glass: it examines the parts closely and explains them clearly, without taking sides."},"objectives":["State the purpose and specifications of an expository essay.","Use the six methods of exposition and identify the seven types of expository essays.","Write an objective, well-organised expository essay within the word limit.","Follow the five-paragraph format and the step-by-step method to write an expository essay under time."],"html":"<p>An <b>expository essay</b> explains, informs or clarifies a topic so that the reader understands it. It is the most important genre in this course: <b>all three Paper 02 essays are expository</b>, and so is the expository option in Paper 032. Even when you analyse an extract in Module 1, you are explaining (the writer's purpose, strategies and credibility), not arguing for a side.</p>\n<h4>Specifications</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>To explain or inform objectively.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Thesis</b></td><td>States what will be explained and previews the parts. No opinion to defend.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Point of view</b></td><td>Third person (no \"I think\", \"I believe\", \"you\").</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tone and register</b></td><td>Formal, neutral, precise. Standard English throughout.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Structure</b></td><td>Introduction (hook, bridge, thesis) → three PEEL body paragraphs, one point each, in thesis order → conclusion (3 Rs).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Evidence</b></td><td>Facts, examples, definitions, cause-and-effect explanation, short quotations from the extract (Modules 1–2).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Transitions</b></td><td>Sequence and consequence: First, Second, In addition, As a result, Finally.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Length</b></td><td>No more than 500 words (Paper 02 Modules 1–2); no more than 300 words (Module 3); 200–250 words plus a 50–100-word justification (Paper 032 Section B).</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>The format: what goes in each paragraph</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Paragraph</th><th>What goes in it</th><th>300 words</th><th>500 words</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Introduction</b></td><td><b>Hook</b> (a striking fact, question or brief scene) → <b>bridge</b> (context; define the key term) → <b>thesis</b> that names the topic and previews your three points in the order you will write them. No opinion.</td><td>~45</td><td>~70</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Body paragraph 1</b></td><td><b>PEEL:</b> <b>P</b>oint (topic sentence: the first point from your thesis) → <b>E</b>vidence (fact, example or short quotation) → <b>E</b>xplain how or why it matters → <b>L</b>ink back to the question.</td><td>~70</td><td>~120</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Body paragraph 2</b></td><td>PEEL for the second point. Open with an ACCESS transition (<i>In addition</i>, <i>Similarly</i>, <i>As a result</i>).</td><td>~70</td><td>~120</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Body paragraph 3</b></td><td>PEEL for the third point, in thesis order.</td><td>~70</td><td>~120</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Conclusion</b></td><td><b>3 Rs:</b> <b>R</b>estate the thesis in new words → <b>R</b>ecap the three points → <b>R</b>esonate (the wider significance). No new points.</td><td>~45</td><td>~70</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"hint\">Five paragraphs, third person, one point per body paragraph. In a Module 1 or 2 analysis, the three points are usually the writer's purpose, strategies and credibility, or whatever the question asks for.</p>\n<h4>Writing it, step by step</h4>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Time guide for a 50-minute essay:</b> steps 1 to 5 (planning) by minute 10 · steps 6 to 8 (writing) by minute 45 · the last step (checking) in the final 5 minutes.</p>\n<ol class=\"ww-steps\">\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Unpack the question</h4><p>Circle the command word (<i>explain</i>, <i>outline</i>, <i>discuss</i>), underline the topic and every limit (<i>THREE</i>, <i>in the Caribbean</i>, <i>for young people</i>), and note the word limit. Every part you underline must be answered.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Choose your method of exposition</h4><p>Decide which method fits the question (DPCCCI): definition, process, cause and effect, comparison and contrast, classification or illustration. The method decides how you order your points.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Brainstorm, then pick three points</h4><p>List every point you can in one minute, then choose the three that are most distinct and easiest to support. Jot one piece of evidence beside each.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Write the thesis</h4><p>One sentence that names the topic and previews the three points in order, without taking a side: <i>\"Sargassum harms Caribbean coastlines in three ways: it deters tourists, entangles fishing gear and releases harmful gas.\"</i></p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Outline in five lines</h4><p>Thesis · Point 1 + evidence · Point 2 + evidence · Point 3 + evidence · closing thought. This is your map; you should be writing by minute 10.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Draft the introduction</h4><p>Hook, bridge, thesis. Keep it short: the marks are in the body.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Draft three PEEL body paragraphs</h4><p>One point per paragraph, in thesis order. Open each with an ACCESS transition and spend most of each paragraph on the <b>Explain</b> step: naming a point earns little; explaining it earns Content marks.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Write the 3R conclusion</h4><p>Restate, recap, resonate. Do not add a new point or an opinion.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Revise, proofread and count</h4><p>Check that every part of the question is answered; remove \"I\", \"you\" and opinion words; fix grammar, spelling, punctuation and tense; count your words and cut to the limit.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h4>The six methods of exposition</h4>\n<p>An expository essay is usually built on one main <b>method</b>, chosen to suit the question. Many essays combine two.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Answers the question…</th><th>Signal words</th><th>Caribbean example topic</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Definition</b></td><td>What is it? What does it mean?</td><td>is defined as, refers to, means</td><td>What is \"code-switching\"?</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Process</b></td><td>How does it happen or how is it done?</td><td>first, next, then, finally</td><td>How a community prepares for a hurricane</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cause and effect</b></td><td>Why does it happen? What results?</td><td>because, as a result, leads to, therefore</td><td>Causes of sargassum influxes; effects of brain drain</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Comparison and contrast</b></td><td>How are they alike or different?</td><td>similarly, whereas, in contrast, both</td><td>Radio versus social media for emergency news</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Classification</b></td><td>What are the types or categories?</td><td>types of, kinds of, categories, fall into</td><td>Types of communication barriers in the workplace</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Illustration (example)</b></td><td>What does it look like in practice?</td><td>for example, for instance, such as</td><td>How Caribbean music carries social commentary</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>The types of expository essays</h4>\n<p>Each method of exposition gives its name to a <b>type</b> of expository essay. A seventh type, <b>problem and solution</b>, joins two methods together. Every type keeps the same core rules: a thesis that previews the points, third person, an objective tone and PEEL body paragraphs. What changes is the question the essay answers and the order of its points.</p>\n<div class=\"ww-grid two\">\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>1. Definition essay</h4><p>Explains what a term, concept or idea means, going well beyond a one-line dictionary entry. It sets out the term's essential features, shows it in use and often says what it is <i>not</i>, so the reader can recognise it.</p><p><b>Question signals:</b> <i>What is meant by…</i>, <i>Define and explain…</i>, <i>Explain the term…</i></p><p><b>Organise it:</b> a clear formal definition in the introduction → one body paragraph on each key feature (or features, examples and common confusions) → conclusion on why the term matters.</p><p class=\"hint\"><b>Model thesis:</b> <i>Code-switching is the movement between two languages or varieties in one conversation, and it can be understood through its forms, its triggers and its functions.</i></p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>2. Process essay</h4><p>Explains how something happens or how something is done, stage by stage in time order. An <b>instructional</b> process tells the reader how to do it; an <b>informational</b> process explains how it happens (how a bill becomes law, how a hurricane forms).</p><p><b>Question signals:</b> <i>Explain how…</i>, <i>Outline the stages…</i>, <i>Describe the steps…</i></p><p><b>Organise it:</b> group the many small steps into three stages, one per body paragraph, in strict chronological order, joined by sequence transitions (<i>First</i>, <i>Next</i>, <i>Once this is done</i>, <i>Finally</i>).</p><p class=\"hint\"><b>Model thesis:</b> <i>Preparing a household for hurricane season involves three stages: securing the home, stocking supplies and planning an evacuation route.</i></p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>3. Cause and effect essay</h4><p>Explains <b>why</b> something happens (causes), <b>what results</b> from it (effects), or both. It may also trace a <b>causal chain</b>, in which one event leads to the next. The skill is to explain the link, not just to list events: something that happened first did not necessarily cause what came after.</p><p><b>Question signals:</b> <i>Why…</i>, <i>Account for…</i>, <i>reasons for</i>, <i>the impact of</i>, <i>the consequences of</i></p><p><b>Organise it:</b> one cause or one effect per body paragraph, usually from the most immediate to the most significant, joined by <i>because</i>, <i>as a result</i>, <i>consequently</i>.</p><p class=\"hint\"><b>Model thesis:</b> <i>Brain drain in small island states stems from limited job opportunities, higher salaries abroad and scholarships that do not require graduates to return.</i></p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>4. Comparison and contrast essay</h4><p>Explains two subjects by setting out how they are <b>alike</b> (comparison) and how they <b>differ</b> (contrast) on the same criteria. Its aim is to help the reader understand both subjects more clearly, not to declare a winner; choosing a winner turns it into an argumentative essay.</p><p><b>Question signals:</b> <i>Compare…</i>, <i>Contrast…</i>, <i>similarities and differences</i>, <i>How does X differ from Y?</i></p><p><b>Organise it:</b> choose two or three criteria, then use the block or point-by-point pattern (explained below), joined by <i>similarly</i>, <i>whereas</i>, <i>in contrast</i>.</p><p class=\"hint\"><b>Model thesis:</b> <i>Radio and social media both carry emergency news in the Caribbean, but they differ in speed, reach and reliability.</i></p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>5. Classification essay</h4><p>Explains a broad topic by sorting it into <b>types</b> or <b>categories</b> and describing each one with examples. Every category must be sorted on the same basis (the <b>principle of classification</b>), and the categories must not overlap.</p><p><b>Question signals:</b> <i>types of</i>, <i>kinds of</i>, <i>forms of</i>, <i>categories of</i>, <i>Identify and explain the different…</i></p><p><b>Organise it:</b> name the basis of sorting in the thesis → one category per body paragraph, each defined, illustrated and explained → conclusion on how the categories relate.</p><p class=\"hint\"><b>Model thesis:</b> <i>Barriers to communication in the workplace fall into three categories: physical, psychological and semantic.</i></p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>6. Illustration (example) essay</h4><p>Explains a general idea by showing it through a few <b>well-chosen, specific examples</b>. Each example is developed and explained, so the reader sees exactly how it proves the general point.</p><p><b>Question signals:</b> <i>Using examples, explain…</i>, <i>Illustrate…</i>, <i>Show how…</i></p><p><b>Organise it:</b> one extended example per body paragraph, usually from the least to the most striking, joined by <i>for example</i>, <i>for instance</i>, <i>a further case is</i>.</p><p class=\"hint\"><b>Model thesis:</b> <i>Caribbean music has long carried social commentary, as calypso, reggae and dancehall each show.</i></p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>7. Problem and solution essay</h4><p>Explains a problem (what it is, what causes it and whom it affects) and then sets out <b>workable solutions</b>, explaining how each one would work. It combines cause and effect with process. It stays expository by explaining the options clearly; urging the reader to act would make it persuasive.</p><p><b>Question signals:</b> <i>Suggest ways…</i>, <i>Explain how X could be reduced</i>, <i>measures that could be taken</i></p><p><b>Organise it:</b> introduction states the problem → one solution per body paragraph, each with how it works and why it fits → conclusion on the combined effect. Module 3 scenario responses and Paper 032 advice pieces often take this shape.</p><p class=\"hint\"><b>Model thesis:</b> <i>Plastic waste at school can be reduced through refill stations, a classroom recycling roster and reusable containers at the canteen.</i></p></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"row\" style=\"margin:10px 0\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn gold\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/funnel.html\" data-id=\"funnel\">🔻 Play Funnel Vision: types, funnels, sample essays and mind maps</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/funnelbuilder.html\" data-id=\"funnelbuilder\">🏗️ Open the Funnel Builder: write your own essay</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/essaymindmap.html\" data-id=\"essaymindmap\">🧠 Open the Essay Mind Map: plan your essay</button></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Which type does the question want?</b> The command word and the key noun usually tell you: <i>why</i> → cause and effect; <i>how</i> → process; <i>types</i> → classification; <i>differ</i> → comparison and contrast; <i>what is meant by</i> → definition; <i>using examples</i> → illustration; <i>ways to reduce</i> → problem and solution. Many essays use one main type and borrow from another, for example a cause and effect essay that opens with a short definition. Name your main type in your plan so that your body paragraphs follow its order.</p>\n<h4>Two ways to organise comparison and contrast</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Block:</b> everything about A, then everything about B. Simple, but the reader must hold A in mind.</li>\n<li><b>Point-by-point:</b> each paragraph compares A and B on one criterion (cost, speed, reach). Usually stronger in an exam because the comparison is explicit.</li></ul>\n<h4>Keep it objective</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Opinionated</b>I think sargassum is the worst thing to ever happen to our beaches and the government should be ashamed.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Expository</b>Sargassum damages Caribbean coastlines in three ways: it deters tourists, entangles fishing gear and releases hydrogen sulphide gas as it decomposes.</div></div>\n<h4>What earns Grade One</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Content (10):</b> every part of the question answered; points explained, not just named; specific examples.</li>\n<li><b>Organization (7):</b> thesis previews the points; paragraphs follow that order; ACCESS transitions; 3R conclusion.</li>\n<li><b>Expression (8):</b> objective third person, precise vocabulary, varied sentences, accurate Standard English.</li></ul>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"Don't Panic, Cats Can Climb Ivy","word":"DPCCCI","letters":[["D","Definition","What is it?"],["P","Process","How does it happen?"],["C","Cause and effect","Why? What results?"],["C","Comparison and contrast","How alike? How different?"],["C","Classification","What types are there?"],["I","Illustration","What does it look like in practice?"]],"extra":"Expository = <b>explain, don't argue</b>. Third person, formal, objective."},"check":[{"q":"Which thesis is expository?","o":["Schools must ban phones immediately.","Phones affect classroom learning in three ways: they speed up research, increase distraction and change how students take notes.","I love my phone.","Should phones be banned?"],"a":1,"why":"It explains effects without arguing for a side and previews three points."},{"q":"\"How a bill becomes law in Barbados\" calls mainly for which method?","o":["Definition","Process","Classification","Illustration"],"a":1,"why":"It asks how something happens step by step, which is process."},{"q":"Which point of view suits an expository essay?","o":["First person: \"I believe…\"","Second person: \"You should…\"","Third person, objective","Any, as long as it is persuasive"],"a":2,"why":"Expository writing stays objective and formal, so it uses the third person."},{"q":"Point-by-point comparison is often stronger in an exam because…","o":["it is shorter","it makes the comparison explicit in every paragraph","it avoids a thesis","it uses more quotations"],"a":1,"why":"Each paragraph compares both subjects on one criterion, so the examiner sees analysis rather than two separate descriptions."}],"task":{"prompt":"Write an expository essay of NO MORE than 300 words: <i>\"Explain THREE ways in which young people in the Caribbean can reduce plastic waste at school.\"</i> Use one main method of exposition and name it at the top.","min":220,"max":300,"checklist":["I named my method of exposition.","My thesis previews three ways and takes no side.","Each body paragraph follows PEEL.","I wrote in the third person and a formal tone.","My conclusion restates, recaps and resonates.","I stayed within 300 words."]}},{"id":"expo-guide","n":11,"icon":"🔎","title":"Writing an Expository Essay: A Detailed Guide","genre":true,"kicker":"Every stage, illustrated, with a model essay","objectives":["Unpack a Communication Studies question and choose a method of exposition.","Plan an expository essay with a brainstorm, a thesis and a five-line outline.","Write a funnel introduction, three PEEL body paragraphs and a 3R conclusion.","Judge a model essay against the 25-mark scheme and adapt the method for Modules 1, 2 and 3."],"html":"<style>.xg-fig svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;background:var(--surface);border-radius:9px}\n.xg-q{background:var(--surface);border:1.5px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:12px 14px}\n.xg-qtext{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:2.1;margin:0 0 8px}\n.xq{border-bottom:3px solid var(--c);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--c) 12%,transparent);padding:1px 3px;border-radius:3px}\n.xq sup{color:var(--c);font-weight:800;margin-left:2px}\n.xg-keys{list-style:none;padding:0;margin:0;display:grid;gap:5px}\n.xg-keys li{display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:baseline}\n.xk{display:inline-grid;place-items:center;min-width:20px;height:20px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--c);color:#fff;font-weight:800;font-size:.75rem;padding:0 4px;flex:none}\n.xg-peel .p1{background:color-mix(in srgb,#2F6FD0 16%,transparent)}.xg-peel .p2{background:color-mix(in srgb,#1C8A4A 16%,transparent)}\n.xg-peel .p3{background:color-mix(in srgb,#7B4FC4 16%,transparent)}.xg-peel .p4{background:color-mix(in srgb,#B7791F 18%,transparent)}\n.xg-peel span{border-radius:4px;padding:1px 2px}\nul.chk{list-style:none;padding-left:0}ul.chk li{margin:4px 0}</style><p>This chapter takes you through an expository essay <b>from the question to the final full stop</b>, with an illustration for every stage. Use it with the chapter before it (<i>The Expository Essay</i>), which sets out the specifications and the six methods of exposition. Here you will watch one Communication Studies essay being built, see exactly what goes in every sentence of it, and learn how the same method changes for Modules 1, 2 and 3.</p>\n<h4>Where expository essays appear in Communication Studies</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Paper</th><th>What you explain</th><th>Length</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Paper 02 · Module 1</b></td><td>An extract: its main point, the writer's purpose, the strategies or techniques used, and the credibility of the source.</td><td>No more than 500 words</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Paper 02 · Module 2</b></td><td>Language in a transcript or extract: features of Creole and Standard English, attitudes to language, language and identity or technology.</td><td>No more than 500 words</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Paper 02 · Module 3</b></td><td>A communication scenario: barriers, channels, audience, context, ethics, or how to communicate a message effectively.</td><td>No more than 300 words</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Paper 032 · Section B</b></td><td>The expository option on your research topic, for a stated audience.</td><td>200–250 words, plus a justification</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every one of these is marked out of <b>25: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8</b>. The shape of the essay stays the same; only the knowledge you bring changes.</p>\n\n<h4>The shape of an expository essay</h4>\n<figure class=\"ww-fig xg-fig\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"The shape of an expository essay\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 520\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The five-paragraph expository essay: an introduction shaped like a funnel ending in the thesis, three PEEL body paragraphs each matching one point in the thesis, and a conclusion shaped like a widening funnel.\"><path d=\"M150 20 H530 L440 110 H240 Z\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 22%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >INTRODUCTION</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Hook  →  Bridge  →  Thesis</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >broad  →  narrow</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"120\" width=\"280\" height=\"34\" rx=\"17\" style=\"fill:var(--gold)\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"142\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#1a1300\" >THESIS: topic + 3 points in order</text><path d=\"M300 154 C 300 175, 120 165, 120 188\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:#2F6FD0;stroke-width:2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#xga0)\"/><defs><marker id=\"xga0\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"4\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z\" style=\"fill:#2F6FD0\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"20\" y=\"192\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" rx=\"12\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,#2F6FD0 10%,transparent);stroke:#2F6FD0;stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#2F6FD0\" >BODY 1: Point 1</text><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"240\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#2F6FD0\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >P</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Point (topic sentence)</text><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"276\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#2F6FD0\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >E</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Evidence</text><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"312\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#2F6FD0\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"317\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >E</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"317\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Explain (longest part)</text><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"348\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#2F6FD0\"/><text x=\"42\" y=\"353\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >L</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"353\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Link to the question</text><path d=\"M340 154 C 340 175, 340 165, 340 188\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:#1C8A4A;stroke-width:2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#xga1)\"/><defs><marker id=\"xga1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"4\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z\" style=\"fill:#1C8A4A\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"240\" y=\"192\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" rx=\"12\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,#1C8A4A 10%,transparent);stroke:#1C8A4A;stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#1C8A4A\" >BODY 2: Point 2</text><circle cx=\"262\" cy=\"240\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#1C8A4A\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >P</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Point (topic sentence)</text><circle cx=\"262\" cy=\"276\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#1C8A4A\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >E</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Evidence</text><circle cx=\"262\" cy=\"312\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#1C8A4A\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"317\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >E</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"317\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Explain (longest part)</text><circle cx=\"262\" cy=\"348\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#1C8A4A\"/><text x=\"262\" y=\"353\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >L</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"353\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Link to the question</text><path d=\"M380 154 C 380 175, 560 165, 560 188\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:#7B4FC4;stroke-width:2.5\" marker-end=\"url(#xga2)\"/><defs><marker id=\"xga2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"4\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z\" style=\"fill:#7B4FC4\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"460\" y=\"192\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" rx=\"12\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,#7B4FC4 10%,transparent);stroke:#7B4FC4;stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"560\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#7B4FC4\" >BODY 3: Point 3</text><circle cx=\"482\" cy=\"240\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#7B4FC4\"/><text x=\"482\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >P</text><text x=\"502\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Point (topic sentence)</text><circle cx=\"482\" cy=\"276\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#7B4FC4\"/><text x=\"482\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >E</text><text x=\"502\" y=\"281\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Evidence</text><circle cx=\"482\" cy=\"312\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#7B4FC4\"/><text x=\"482\" y=\"317\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >E</text><text x=\"502\" y=\"317\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Explain (longest part)</text><circle cx=\"482\" cy=\"348\" r=\"12\" style=\"fill:#7B4FC4\"/><text x=\"482\" y=\"353\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >L</text><text x=\"502\" y=\"353\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Link to the question</text><path d=\"M240 410 H440 L530 500 H150 Z\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 22%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"436\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >CONCLUSION</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"458\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Restate  →  Recap  →  Resonate (3 Rs)</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"482\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >narrow  →  broad</text></svg><figcaption><b>Illustration 1</b> The five-paragraph expository essay. The introduction narrows like a funnel to the thesis; the thesis previews three points; each point gets its own PEEL paragraph in the same order; the conclusion widens out again with the 3 Rs.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Keep this picture in your head. If you can draw it, you can plan any expository essay in under ten minutes.</p>\n\n<h4>Stage 1 · Unpack the question</h4>\n<p>Most lost marks begin here: candidates answer the question they hoped for, not the one on the paper. Read the question twice and mark six things.</p>\n<figure class=\"ww-fig xg-fig\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Unpacking an exam question\"><div class=\"xg-q\"><p class=\"xg-qtext\"><span class=\"xq\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\">Explain<sup>1</sup></span> <span class=\"xq\" style=\"--c:#2F6FD0\">THREE<sup>2</sup></span> <span class=\"xq\" style=\"--c:#1C8A4A\">barriers that may prevent effective communication<sup>3</sup></span> <span class=\"xq\" style=\"--c:#7B4FC4\">between a manager and staff<sup>4</sup></span> <span class=\"xq\" style=\"--c:#B7791F\">in a Caribbean workplace<sup>5</sup></span>. <span class=\"xq\" style=\"--c:#586076\">(No more than 300 words)<sup>6</sup></span></p><ul class=\"xg-keys\"><li><span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#C0392B\">1</span><b>Command word:</b> Explain = make clear how or why. Not \"list\", not \"argue\".</li><li><span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#2F6FD0\">2</span><b>Number:</b> Exactly three. Two loses Content marks; four wastes words.</li><li><span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#1C8A4A\">3</span><b>Topic:</b> Barriers to effective communication (Module 3 knowledge).</li><li><span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#7B4FC4\">4</span><b>Participants:</b> Manager and staff: every point must involve this relationship.</li><li><span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#B7791F\">5</span><b>Context:</b> A Caribbean workplace: use regional, specific examples.</li><li><span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#586076\">6</span><b>Limit:</b> 300 words: about 45 + 70 + 70 + 70 + 45.</li></ul></div><figcaption><b>Illustration 2</b> A Module 3 question unpacked. Each coloured part of the question tells you something you must do.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Command words decide your genre.</b> <i>Explain, describe, outline, discuss, examine, account for</i> and <i>analyse</i> all call for exposition. If the question says <i>argue</i>, <i>to what extent do you agree</i> or <i>persuade</i>, you are in a different genre.</p>\n\n<h4>Stage 2 · Choose a method and brainstorm</h4>\n<p>Decide which method of exposition the question needs (DPCCCI). \"Explain THREE barriers\" is <b>classification</b> (types of barrier) combined with <b>cause and effect</b> (how each one causes communication to fail). Then brainstorm for one minute: write down every idea, without judging. Finally, circle the three that are most distinct and easiest to support with a specific example.</p>\n<figure class=\"ww-fig xg-fig\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"A brainstorm cluster\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 330\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A brainstorm cluster: six possible barriers around the topic. Three are circled as the strongest: language and register, physical noise, and status and hierarchy.\"><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"70\" style=\"stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:3\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"560\" y2=\"70\" style=\"stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:3\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"560\" y2=\"250\" style=\"stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:3\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"250\" style=\"stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"300\" style=\"stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"165\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"28\" style=\"stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:2\"/><ellipse cx=\"340\" cy=\"165\" rx=\"130\" ry=\"44\" style=\"fill:var(--gold)\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#1a1300\" >Barriers between manager</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#1a1300\" >and staff</text><rect x=\"24.0\" y=\"50\" width=\"192\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface)\"/><rect x=\"24.0\" y=\"50\" width=\"192\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 24%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:3;stroke-dasharray:0\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"75\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >✓ Language and register</text><rect x=\"485.0\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface)\"/><rect x=\"485.0\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 24%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:3;stroke-dasharray:0\"/><text x=\"560\" y=\"75\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >✓ Physical noise</text><rect x=\"468.0\" y=\"230\" width=\"184\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface)\"/><rect x=\"468.0\" y=\"230\" width=\"184\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 24%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:3;stroke-dasharray:0\"/><text x=\"560\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >✓ Status and hierarchy</text><rect x=\"45.0\" y=\"230\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface)\"/><rect x=\"45.0\" y=\"230\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:1.5;stroke-dasharray:5 4\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >Poor timing</text><rect x=\"248.0\" y=\"280\" width=\"184\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface)\"/><rect x=\"248.0\" y=\"280\" width=\"184\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:1.5;stroke-dasharray:5 4\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"305\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >Information overload</text><rect x=\"224.0\" y=\"8\" width=\"232\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface)\"/><rect x=\"224.0\" y=\"8\" width=\"232\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--line);stroke-width:1.5;stroke-dasharray:5 4\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"33\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >Wrong channel (email only)</text></svg><figcaption><b>Illustration 3</b> A one-minute brainstorm. Six possible barriers came up; the three circled ones are the most different from each other and the easiest to explain with Caribbean examples.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<p class=\"warn\"><b>Avoid overlapping points.</b> \"Poor timing\" and \"information overload\" are both really about how a message is delivered, so choosing both would give you two paragraphs saying the same thing.</p>\n\n<h4>Stage 3 · Write the thesis</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-formula\"><span><b>Topic</b><br><small>what the essay is about</small></span><i>+</i><span><b>Controlling idea</b><br><small>what you will show</small></span><i>+</i><span><b>Preview</b><br><small>your three points, in order</small></span></div>\n<p class=\"passage\">In Caribbean workplaces, <b>three barriers</b> often block that understanding: <b>differences in language</b>, <b>physical noise</b> and <b>the gap in status between managers and staff</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Weak (announces)</b>In this essay I will talk about barriers to communication.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Strong (previews)</b>In Caribbean workplaces, three barriers often block understanding: differences in language, physical noise and the gap in status between managers and staff.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Weak (argues)</b>Managers in the Caribbean are terrible communicators and must be retrained.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Strong (explains)</b>Three barriers often prevent managers and staff from understanding each other.</div></div>\n\n<h4>Stage 4 · Outline in five lines</h4>\n<p>Spend two minutes on this. It is your map, and it guarantees that your essay is organised (7 marks).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Thesis</b></td><td>Language · physical noise · status gap</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Body 1</b></td><td>Language: Creole/SE + Spanish, Dutch, Papiamentu in SXM → message decoded wrongly</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Body 2</b></td><td>Noise: hotel kitchen, port warehouse → half a safety warning → danger</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Body 3</b></td><td>Status: staff afraid to ask → no feedback → one-way communication</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Close</b></td><td>Recognising barriers = first step to clear workplaces</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h4>Stage 5 · Write the introduction: hook, bridge, thesis</h4>\n<figure class=\"ww-fig xg-fig\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"The introduction as a funnel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 300\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An introduction as a funnel: a wide hook, a narrower bridge and a narrow thesis.\"><rect x=\"40.0\" y=\"10\" width=\"600\" height=\"88\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 14%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--gold)\" >HOOK</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >When a supervisor at a Philipsburg resort changes the roster by email and half the</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >staff still arrive at the old time, the problem is not effort but communication.</text><rect x=\"105.0\" y=\"106\" width=\"470\" height=\"88\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 26%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--gold)\" >BRIDGE</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >A message succeeds only when the receiver understands it as the</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"161\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >sender intended.</text><rect x=\"170.0\" y=\"202\" width=\"340\" height=\"88\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 38%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--gold)\" >THESIS</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >In Caribbean workplaces, three barriers often</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"257\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >block that understanding: differences in</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"272\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >language, physical noise and the gap in status</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"287\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >between managers and staff.</text></svg><figcaption><b>Illustration 4</b> The introduction narrows from a wide hook, through a bridge, to a precise thesis. Each layer is one sentence.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"ww-grid two\"><div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>Hooks that work</h4><ul><li>A specific scene: \"When a supervisor at a Philipsburg resort…\"</li><li>A striking fact: \"Sint Maarten is home to people of more than 100 nationalities.\"</li><li>A short question: \"Why do clear instructions so often go wrong?\"</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>Hooks to avoid</h4><ul><li>Dictionary definitions: \"Webster's defines communication as…\"</li><li>Announcements: \"This essay will discuss…\"</li><li>Sweeping claims: \"Since the beginning of time, humans have communicated.\"</li></ul></div></div>\n\n<h4>Stage 6 · Write three PEEL body paragraphs</h4>\n<figure class=\"ww-fig xg-fig\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"How a PEEL paragraph is shared out\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 120\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A bar showing how a 70-word PEEL paragraph is shared out: point 15 percent, evidence 25 percent, explanation 45 percent, link 15 percent.\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"92.0\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:#2F6FD0\"/><text x=\"66.0\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >P · Point</text><text x=\"66.0\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >15% · ~10 words</text><text x=\"66.0\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >One sentence</text><rect x=\"116.0\" y=\"20\" width=\"156.0\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:#1C8A4A\"/><text x=\"194.0\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >E · Evidence</text><text x=\"194.0\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >25% · ~18 words</text><text x=\"194.0\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >Fact, example, quotation</text><rect x=\"276.0\" y=\"20\" width=\"284.0\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:#7B4FC4\"/><text x=\"418.0\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >E · Explain</text><text x=\"418.0\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >45% · ~32 words</text><text x=\"418.0\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >How? Why? So what?</text><rect x=\"564.0\" y=\"20\" width=\"92.0\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:#B7791F\"/><text x=\"610.0\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >L · Link</text><text x=\"610.0\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >15% · ~10 words</text><text x=\"610.0\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\" >Back to the question</text></svg><figcaption><b>Illustration 5</b> How the words in a 70-word body paragraph should be shared out. The Explain step is the longest because that is where Content marks are earned.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<p>Here is one body paragraph from the model essay, coloured by PEEL stage:</p>\n<p class=\"passage xg-peel\"><span class=\"p1\">Physical noise interferes with the transmission of messages.</span> <span class=\"p2\">In a busy hotel kitchen or a port warehouse, machinery, music and conversation drown out instructions.</span> <span class=\"p3\">A worker who catches only half of a safety warning will act on an incomplete message.</span> <span class=\"p4\">Physical noise therefore does not only delay communication; it can make it dangerous.</span></p>\n<p class=\"hint\"><span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#2F6FD0\">P</span> Point · <span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#1C8A4A\">E</span> Evidence · <span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#7B4FC4\">E</span> Explain · <span class=\"xk\" style=\"--c:#B7791F\">L</span> Link</p>\n<p>Start the second and third paragraphs with an <b>ACCESS</b> transition so the examiner can follow your order: <i>The first barrier… In addition… Finally…</i> Keep every paragraph to <b>one point</b>; if you find a second idea creeping in, it belongs in another paragraph or not at all.</p>\n\n<h4>Stage 7 · Close with the 3 Rs</h4>\n<figure class=\"ww-fig xg-fig\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"The conclusion as a widening funnel\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 200\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A conclusion as a widening funnel: restate, recap, resonate.\"><rect x=\"170.0\" y=\"8\" width=\"340\" height=\"56\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 34%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"32\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--gold)\" >RESTATE</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >the thesis in new words</text><rect x=\"105.0\" y=\"72\" width=\"470\" height=\"56\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 24%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--gold)\" >RECAP</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >the three points, briefly, in order</text><rect x=\"40.0\" y=\"136\" width=\"600\" height=\"56\" rx=\"10\" style=\"fill:color-mix(in srgb,var(--gold) 14%,transparent);stroke:var(--gold);stroke-width:2\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--gold)\" >RESONATE</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >why it matters beyond the essay</text></svg><figcaption><b>Illustration 6</b> The conclusion reverses the introduction: it starts with the thesis and widens to a final thought.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<p class=\"warn\"><b>Never add a new point in the conclusion</b>, and never end with \"In conclusion, I have shown…\". A conclusion that only repeats the introduction word for word also loses Organization marks.</p>\n\n<h4>The complete model essay</h4>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Question:</b> Explain THREE barriers that may prevent effective communication between a manager and staff in a Caribbean workplace. (No more than 300 words)</p>\n<div class=\"gn-ex\"><h4 class=\"gn-title\">Barriers to Communication in the Caribbean Workplace</h4><p><span class=\"xa\">When a supervisor at a Philipsburg resort changes the roster by email and half the staff still arrive at the old time, the problem is not effort but communication.</span><span class=\"xn\">1</span> <span class=\"xa\">A message succeeds only when the receiver understands it as the sender intended.</span><span class=\"xn\">2</span> <span class=\"xa\">In Caribbean workplaces, three barriers often block that understanding: differences in language, physical noise and the gap in status between managers and staff.</span><span class=\"xn\">3</span></p><p><span class=\"xa\">The first barrier is language.</span><span class=\"xn\">4</span> Many Caribbean workers move between Creole and Standard English, and <span class=\"xa\">in Sint Maarten many also speak Spanish, Dutch or Papiamentu.</span><span class=\"xn\">5</span> A manager who writes in formal, technical English may lose staff who are more comfortable in another variety, while a casual Creole phrase may confuse a newly arrived colleague. <span class=\"xa\">When sender and receiver do not share the same code, the message is decoded inaccurately, so instructions are followed incorrectly.</span><span class=\"xn\">6</span></p><p><span class=\"xa\">In addition,</span><span class=\"xn\">7</span> physical noise interferes with the transmission of messages. In a busy hotel kitchen or a port warehouse, machinery, music and conversation drown out instructions. A worker who catches only half of a safety warning will act on an incomplete message. <span class=\"xa\">Physical noise therefore does not only delay communication; it can make it dangerous.</span><span class=\"xn\">8</span></p><p>Finally, the difference in status between managers and staff creates a psychological barrier. Junior employees may be afraid to ask questions or admit confusion because they fear appearing incompetent. As a result, the manager receives no feedback and assumes the message was clear. <span class=\"xa\">Without feedback, communication becomes one-way, and errors are discovered only after they have been made.</span><span class=\"xn\">9</span></p><p><span class=\"xa\">Clearly, communication in the Caribbean workplace can fail long before a message is acted on.</span><span class=\"xn\">10</span> <span class=\"xa\">Differences in language, physical noise and the gap in status each distort or block meaning.</span><span class=\"xn\">11</span> <span class=\"xa\">Recognising these barriers is the first step towards workplaces where every message arrives as it was intended.</span><span class=\"xn\">12</span></p></div>\n<p class=\"hint\">299 words. Numbers match the notes below.</p>\n<ol class=\"gn-notes\"><li><span><b>Hook:</b> a specific Caribbean scene the reader recognises, not a dictionary definition.</span></li><li><span><b>Bridge:</b> states the key idea (effective communication) that connects the hook to the thesis.</span></li><li><span><b>Thesis:</b> names the topic and previews three points in the order the body will follow. No opinion.</span></li><li><span><b>Topic sentence (Point):</b> one point, taken straight from the thesis.</span></li><li><span><b>Evidence:</b> a specific regional example, not a vague \"people speak differently\".</span></li><li><span><b>Explain + Link:</b> uses Module 3 vocabulary (code, decoding) to show <i>why</i> this is a barrier, then links back to the question.</span></li><li><span><b>ACCESS transition</b> (addition) signals the second point.</span></li><li><span><b>Explain:</b> goes beyond the example to its consequence (\"it can make it dangerous\").</span></li><li><span><b>Link:</b> connects the point to feedback, a key element of the communication process.</span></li><li><span><b>Restate:</b> the thesis idea in new words.</span></li><li><span><b>Recap:</b> the three points, in the same order.</span></li><li><span><b>Resonate:</b> a forward-looking final thought. No new point.</span></li></ol>\n\n<h4>How the model would be marked</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>What the examiner looks for</th><th>How the model earns it</th><th>Mark</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Content</b></td><td>All parts of the question answered; points explained, not just named; specific examples.</td><td>Three distinct barriers, each tied to the manager–staff relationship and explained with Module 3 terms (code, decoding, feedback) and Caribbean examples.</td><td class=\"n\">9 / 10</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Organization</b></td><td>Clear thesis; one point per paragraph in a logical order; transitions; a conclusion that closes.</td><td>The thesis previews the body order exactly; ACCESS transitions; 3R conclusion.</td><td class=\"n\">7 / 7</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Expression</b></td><td>Accurate Standard English; varied sentences; precise, formal vocabulary.</td><td>Objective third person, varied sentence openings, precise terms. One or two sentences could be tighter.</td><td class=\"n\">7 / 8</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Total: 23 / 25</b>, well inside the Grade One band. To reach full Content marks, the writer could suggest briefly how one barrier might be reduced, if the question invited it.</p>\n\n<h4>The same method in Modules 1 and 2</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-grid two\"><div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>Module 1 · analysing an extract</h4><p>The \"three points\" usually come from the question: for example the writer's <b>purpose</b>, two <b>strategies</b> and the <b>credibility</b> of the source.</p><p class=\"passage\"><i>Thesis:</i> In the extract, the writer aims to persuade parents to limit screen time, using vivid anecdote and statistics, although the absence of a named source weakens the extract's credibility.</p><p>Each PEEL paragraph quotes <b>briefly</b> from the extract (the Evidence) and explains the <b>effect</b> on the reader (the Explain).</p></div>\n<div class=\"ww-card\"><h4>Module 2 · language in a transcript</h4><p>The points are usually <b>language features</b> or <b>attitudes</b>: for example, two Creole features and the speaker's attitude to them.</p><p class=\"passage\"><i>Thesis:</i> The speaker's Creole is marked by the absence of the plural marker and the use of \"fi\" as an infinitive particle, and her switch to Standard English when addressing the officer reveals her awareness of prestige.</p><p>Evidence is a short quotation from the transcript; the Explain step names the feature and its function.</p></div></div>\n\n<h4>Common mistakes and how to fix them</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Naming without explaining</b>Another barrier is noise. Noise is very bad in workplaces.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Explained</b>A worker who catches only half of a safety warning will act on an incomplete message, so noise can make communication dangerous.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Slipping into opinion</b>I personally think managers should be nicer to their staff.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Objective</b>When staff fear appearing incompetent, they stop asking questions and the manager loses feedback.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Thesis and body out of order</b>Thesis: language, noise, status. Body: status, language, noise.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Matching order</b>Thesis: language, noise, status. Body: language → noise → status.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Vague evidence</b>Some people speak differently.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Specific evidence</b>In Sint Maarten many workers also speak Spanish, Dutch or Papiamentu.</div></div>\n\n<h4>Managing your 50 minutes</h4>\n<figure class=\"ww-fig xg-fig\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fifty-minute time plan\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 150\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A 50-minute timeline: 10 minutes planning, 35 minutes writing, 5 minutes proofreading.\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"49.2\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:#2F6FD0\"/><text x=\"44.6\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >4′</text><text x=\"48\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Unpack, brainstorm</text><rect x=\"71.2\" y=\"40\" width=\"74.8\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:#3B8BD9\"/><text x=\"108.6\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >6′</text><text x=\"108.6\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Thesis, outline</text><rect x=\"148.0\" y=\"40\" width=\"74.8\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:#1C8A4A\"/><text x=\"185.4\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >6′</text><text x=\"185.4\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Introduction</text><rect x=\"224.8\" y=\"40\" width=\"100.4\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:#7B4FC4\"/><text x=\"275.0\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >8′</text><text x=\"275.0\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Body 1</text><rect x=\"327.20000000000005\" y=\"40\" width=\"100.4\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:#8E63CF\"/><text x=\"377.40000000000003\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >8′</text><text x=\"377.40000000000003\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Body 2</text><rect x=\"429.6\" y=\"40\" width=\"100.4\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:#A27BDB\"/><text x=\"479.8\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >8′</text><text x=\"479.8\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Body 3</text><rect x=\"532.0\" y=\"40\" width=\"62.0\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:#B7791F\"/><text x=\"563.0\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >5′</text><text x=\"563.0\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Conclusion</text><rect x=\"596.0\" y=\"40\" width=\"62.0\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:#C0392B\"/><text x=\"627.0\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:#fff\" >5′</text><text x=\"627.0\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >Proofread</text><line x1=\"22.0\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"144.0\" y2=\"28\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink);stroke-width:1.5\"/><text x=\"84.0\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >PLAN · 10 min</text><line x1=\"150.0\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"592.0\" y2=\"28\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink);stroke-width:1.5\"/><text x=\"372.0\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >WRITE · 35 min</text><line x1=\"598.0\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"656.0\" y2=\"28\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink);stroke-width:1.5\"/><text x=\"628.0\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"800\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\" >CHECK · 5</text></svg><figcaption><b>Illustration 7</b> A 50-minute plan: ten minutes to plan, thirty-five to write and five to proofread. Never skip the last five minutes: careless errors cost Expression marks.</figcaption></figure>\n\n\n<h4>Final checklist</h4>\n<ul class=\"chk\"><li>☐ I answered the command word, the number and every limit in the question.</li>\n<li>☐ My thesis names the topic and previews my points in order, with no opinion.</li>\n<li>☐ Each body paragraph has one point and follows PEEL, with the Explain step the longest.</li>\n<li>☐ My evidence is specific (named places, quotations, data), not vague.</li>\n<li>☐ ACCESS transitions open paragraphs 2 and 3.</li>\n<li>☐ My conclusion restates, recaps and resonates, with no new point.</li>\n<li>☐ I wrote in the third person, in formal Standard English.</li>\n<li>☐ I am within the word limit.</li></ul>\n","mnemonic":{"phrase":"Hook, Bridge, Thesis · PEEL × 3 · Three Rs","word":"HBT-PEEL-RRR","letters":[["H","Hook","A specific scene, fact or question."],["B","Bridge","Connect the hook to the topic."],["T","Thesis","Topic + controlling idea + three points in order."],["P","Point","One point per paragraph."],["E","Evidence","Specific: names, quotations, data."],["E","Explain","The longest part: how, why, so what."],["L","Link","Back to the question."],["R","Restate","The thesis in new words."],["R","Recap","The three points, in order."],["R","Resonate","Why it matters. No new point."]],"extra":"Plan 10 minutes · write 30 · check 10."},"check":[{"q":"Which command word calls for an expository essay?","o":["Argue","To what extent do you agree","Explain","Persuade"],"a":2,"why":"\"Explain\" asks you to make something clear, without taking sides, which is exposition."},{"q":"In a PEEL paragraph, which part should usually be the longest?","o":["Point","Evidence","Explain","Link"],"a":2,"why":"Explaining how and why your evidence supports the point is where Content marks are earned."},{"q":"Your thesis previews language, noise and status. In what order should the body paragraphs appear?","o":["Any order","Language, noise, status","Status, noise, language","The strongest point last, whatever the thesis says"],"a":1,"why":"The body must follow the order promised in the thesis; that is what earns Organization marks."},{"q":"Which is the best hook for a Communication Studies expository essay?","o":["\"Webster's Dictionary defines communication as…\"","\"This essay will discuss barriers to communication.\"","\"When a supervisor changes the roster by email and half the staff arrive at the old time, the problem is communication.\"","\"Since the beginning of time, humans have talked.\""],"a":2,"why":"A specific, recognisable scene draws the reader in and leads naturally to the topic."},{"q":"Which sentence belongs in a 3R conclusion?","o":["\"A fourth barrier is poor timing.\"","\"Differences in language, physical noise and the gap in status each distort meaning.\"","\"I believe managers should be fired.\"","\"In this essay I have talked about barriers.\""],"a":1,"why":"It recaps the three points in order. New points, opinions and announcements do not belong in a conclusion."}],"task":{"prompt":"Using every stage in this chapter, write an expository essay of NO MORE than 300 words: <i>\"Explain THREE ways in which social media has changed how young people in the Caribbean receive news.\"</i> Before you write, unpack the question, brainstorm, write your thesis and a five-line outline.","min":220,"max":300,"checklist":["I unpacked the command word, the number and the context.","I brainstormed and chose three distinct points.","My thesis previews the three points in order, with no opinion.","My introduction moves from hook to bridge to thesis.","Each body paragraph follows PEEL, with Explain the longest part.","I used ACCESS transitions and specific Caribbean evidence.","My conclusion restates, recaps and resonates.","I stayed within 300 words."]}},{"id":"narrative","n":12,"icon":"🔥","title":"The Narrative Essay","genre":true,"kicker":"Tell a story that means something","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W11.jpg","w":1000,"h":667,"alt":"A group of people sitting around a campfire at night among trees.","by":"Mike Erskine","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/people-having-a-bonfire-S_VbdMTsdiA","cap":"People have gathered to hear stories for as long as there have been fires to sit around. A good narrative makes listeners lean closer and remember the lesson long after the fire goes out."},"objectives":["State the specifications of a narrative essay.","Plan a plot with the narrative arc and SWBST.","Use dialogue, sensory detail and \"show, don't tell\" to reveal a theme.","Follow the narrative format and the step-by-step method to write a story under time."],"html":"<p>A <b>narrative essay</b> tells a story, real or imagined, with a clear sequence of events, believable characters and a <b>point</b>: an insight or theme the story reveals. It is assessed for the same three criteria as other essays, but Content rewards a well-shaped plot and vivid detail, and Organization rewards a clear arc and paragraphing.</p>\n<h4>Specifications</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>To entertain and to reveal an insight through experience.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Point of view</b></td><td>First person (\"I\") for personal narratives, or third person (\"she\", \"they\"). Keep it consistent.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tense</b></td><td>Usually past tense, used consistently.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Structure</b></td><td>The narrative arc: exposition → rising action → climax → falling action → resolution.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Thesis</b></td><td>A controlling insight, often implied, hinted at early or stated in the last lines.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Language</b></td><td>Standard English narration; Creole is welcome inside dialogue to show character and setting. Sensory detail, strong verbs, varied sentences.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Paragraphing</b></td><td>New paragraph for each change of time, place or speaker; each new speaker on a new line.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>The format: what goes in each paragraph</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Paragraph</th><th>What goes in it</th><th>300 words</th><th>500 words</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Opening (exposition)</b></td><td>A <b>hook</b> that drops the reader into the moment (action, dialogue or a sensory image) → who, where and when → a hint of the problem. If the question gives an opening sentence, use it word for word.</td><td>~45</td><td>~70</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Rising action</b></td><td>Two or three events that build the conflict, each one raising the stakes. Use sensory detail and at least one line of correctly punctuated dialogue. This may take two paragraphs.</td><td>~90</td><td>~160</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Climax</b></td><td>The turning point: the moment of greatest tension or decision. Slow down here and <b>show</b> feelings through actions and senses.</td><td>~60</td><td>~100</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Falling action</b></td><td>The consequences of the climax unfold.</td><td>~45</td><td>~80</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Resolution</b></td><td>The conflict settles. The last lines reveal the <b>insight</b> (theme): what the narrator learned or how they changed. If the question gives a closing line, end with it.</td><td>~60</td><td>~90</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"hint\">Start a new paragraph for each change of time, place or speaker. Keep one point of view and one tense throughout.</p>\n<h4>Writing it, step by step</h4>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Time guide for a 50-minute essay:</b> steps 1 to 5 (planning) by minute 10 · steps 6 to 8 (writing) by minute 45 · the last step (checking) in the final 5 minutes.</p>\n<ol class=\"ww-steps\">\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Unpack the prompt</h4><p>Note any sentence you must begin or end with (copy it exactly), the point of view if one is set, and the word limit.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Decide the insight first</h4><p>Before you plan events, decide what the story will reveal: <i>\"A calm voice can shelter people as well as a roof.\"</i> Every event should lead towards it.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Plan the plot with SWBST</h4><p>In one line: <b>S</b>omebody · <b>W</b>anted · <b>B</b>ut · <b>S</b>o · <b>T</b>hen.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Map SWBST onto the arc</h4><p><i>Somebody + Wanted</i> = exposition · <i>But</i> = rising action · <i>So</i> = climax · <i>Then</i> = falling action and resolution. Give the climax the most words.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Fix point of view, tense and setting</h4><p>First person (\"I\") or third person (\"she\"), usually past tense. Pick one setting you can describe through sight, sound and smell.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Write the opening</h4><p>Start close to the action, not with the alarm clock. Hook, characters, setting, and a hint of trouble within the first three sentences.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Build the rising action</h4><p>Two or three events that make the problem worse. Show, don't tell; use dialogue to reveal character, with a new paragraph for each new speaker.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Write the climax, then resolve</h4><p>Slow the climax down with detail. Then resolve the conflict fully (not in one rushed sentence) and let the final lines reveal the insight. Never end with \"…and I woke up.\"</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Revise, proofread and count</h4><p>Check tense consistency, dialogue punctuation and paragraphing; make sure narration is in Standard English (Creole belongs inside dialogue); count your words.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h4>The narrative arc</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-arc\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A rising and falling line: exposition, rising action, climax at the peak, falling action and resolution.\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 200\" width=\"100%\"><polyline points=\"20,170 140,160 300,90 380,30 470,110 620,150\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--gold)\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><g font-size=\"13\" fill=\"var(--ink)\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"70\" y=\"192\">Exposition</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"150\">Rising action</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"20\">Climax</text><text x=\"500\" y=\"90\">Falling action</text><text x=\"585\" y=\"185\">Resolution</text></g><g fill=\"var(--gold)\"><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"170\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"380\" cy=\"30\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"620\" cy=\"150\" r=\"5\"/></g></svg></div>\n<ul><li><b>Exposition:</b> who, where, when; a hint of the problem. Keep it short.</li>\n<li><b>Rising action:</b> the conflict builds through two or three events.</li>\n<li><b>Climax:</b> the turning point, the moment of greatest tension or decision.</li>\n<li><b>Falling action:</b> the consequences unfold.</li>\n<li><b>Resolution:</b> the conflict settles and the character (or narrator) has changed or learned something.</li></ul>\n<h4>Plan the plot in one line: SWBST</h4>\n<p><b>Somebody</b> (character) <b>Wanted</b> (goal) <b>But</b> (conflict) <b>So</b> (action) <b>Then</b> (resolution).</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><i>Twelve-year-old Kezia</i> <b>wanted</b> <i>to keep her younger brothers calm during the storm,</i> <b>but</b> <i>the power failed and the radio died,</i> <b>so</b> <i>her grandmother began telling stories in the dark,</i> <b>then</b> <i>Kezia realised that a calm voice can shelter people as well as a roof.</i></p>\n<h4>Show, don't tell</h4>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Tells</b>I was very scared.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Shows</b>My hands would not stop shaking, and I counted the seconds between each crack of thunder.</div></div>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Tells</b>Granny was calm.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Shows</b>Granny struck a match, settled into her rocking chair and said, \"Come, let me tell you about the time Anancy tried to catch the wind.\"</div></div>\n<h4>Using dialogue correctly</h4>\n<ul><li>Put spoken words inside quotation marks; punctuation goes inside the closing mark: \"Where is the torch?\" she asked.</li>\n<li>Start a new paragraph for each new speaker.</li>\n<li>Use dialogue to reveal character, not to report ordinary greetings.</li>\n<li>Creole in dialogue is authentic; keep the narration around it in Standard English.</li></ul>\n<h4>Common weaknesses</h4>\n<ul><li>A long build-up and a rushed ending (the resolution squeezed into one sentence).</li>\n<li>A list of events (\"and then… and then…\") with no conflict.</li>\n<li>Tense shifts between past and present.</li>\n<li>Endings that cancel the story (\"…and I woke up\").</li></ul>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"Clever Cats Sit Purring Together","word":"CCSPT","letters":[["C","Characters","Few, believable, revealed by action and speech."],["C","Conflict","The problem that drives the story."],["S","Setting","Where and when, through the senses."],["P","Plot","Exposition → rising action → climax → falling action → resolution."],["T","Theme","The insight the story reveals."]],"extra":"Plan the plot with <b>SWBST</b>: Somebody · Wanted · But · So · Then."},"check":[{"q":"Which part of the narrative arc is the turning point?","o":["Exposition","Rising action","Climax","Resolution"],"a":2,"why":"The climax is the moment of greatest tension, after which the conflict begins to resolve."},{"q":"Which sentence shows rather than tells?","o":["He was angry.","He was very, very angry.","He slammed the door so hard the louvres rattled.","He felt anger."],"a":2,"why":"The action lets the reader infer the anger."},{"q":"Where is Creole most appropriate in a narrative?","o":["In the narration","In the dialogue of characters","In the title only","Nowhere"],"a":1,"why":"Creole in dialogue builds authentic characters and setting; the narration stays in Standard English."},{"q":"In SWBST, the B stands for…","o":["Beginning","But (the conflict)","Background","Building"],"a":1,"why":"\"But\" introduces the obstacle that creates conflict."}],"task":{"prompt":"Write a narrative of NO MORE than 300 words that begins: <i>\"The message was only four words long, but it changed my whole afternoon.\"</i> Plan it first with SWBST.","min":220,"max":300,"checklist":["I planned with SWBST.","My story follows the narrative arc to a clear resolution.","I used at least one piece of correctly punctuated dialogue.","I showed feelings through action and detail.","My tense and point of view are consistent.","The ending reveals an insight."]}},{"id":"argumentative","n":13,"icon":"⚖️","title":"The Argumentative Essay","genre":true,"kicker":"Reason your way to a position","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W12.jpg","w":1000,"h":667,"alt":"An antique brass balance scale with two pans.","by":"Piret Ilver","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-and-beige-weighing-scale-98MbUldcDJY","cap":"A brass balance weighs both sides before it settles. An argumentative essay does the same: it weighs the opposing view fairly, then shows why its own side is heavier."},"objectives":["State the specifications of an argumentative essay.","Build each argument with Claim, Evidence and Reasoning.","Write a fair counter-argument and a convincing rebuttal.","Follow the argumentative format and the step-by-step method to write an argumentative essay under time."],"html":"<p>An <b>argumentative essay</b> takes a clear position on a debatable issue and defends it mainly through <b>logic and evidence</b>, while dealing fairly with the opposing view. Its aim is to win the reader's <b>agreement</b> through reasoning.</p>\n<h4>Specifications</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>To convince a reader that a position is reasonable and correct.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Thesis</b></td><td>A clear, debatable claim with a preview of the reasons. No sitting on the fence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Main appeal</b></td><td>Logos (logic and evidence), supported by ethos (credibility). Emotion is used sparingly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Point of view and tone</b></td><td>Usually third person; formal, measured and respectful of the other side.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Structure</b></td><td>Introduction → two reasons (PEEL with claim, evidence, reasoning) → counter-argument and rebuttal → conclusion.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Transitions</b></td><td>Furthermore, Moreover, Admittedly, Critics argue that, However, Nevertheless, Therefore.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>The format: what goes in each paragraph</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Paragraph</th><th>What goes in it</th><th>300 words</th><th>500 words</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Introduction</b></td><td><b>Hook</b> → brief <b>background</b> on the issue and why it is debated → <b>thesis</b> that takes a clear side and previews your reasons.</td><td>~45</td><td>~70</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Reason 1</b></td><td>A PEEL paragraph built with <b>CER</b>: <b>C</b>laim (topic sentence) → <b>E</b>vidence (fact, example, expert view) → <b>R</b>easoning (why the evidence proves the claim) → link to the thesis.</td><td>~70</td><td>~100</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Reason 2</b></td><td>CER for your second reason. Open with <i>Furthermore</i> or <i>Moreover</i>.</td><td>~70</td><td>~100</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Reason 3 (500 words only)</b></td><td>CER for a third reason. In a 300-word essay, leave this out.</td><td>—</td><td>~100</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Counter-argument and rebuttal</b></td><td><b>Concede</b> the strongest opposing view fairly (<i>Admittedly…</i>, <i>Critics argue that…</i>) → <b>rebut</b> it with logic or evidence (<i>However…</i>, <i>Nevertheless…</i>).</td><td>~70</td><td>~90</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Conclusion</b></td><td><b>3 Rs:</b> restate your position in new words → recap the reasons → resonate (why the issue matters). No new evidence.</td><td>~40</td><td>~40</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"hint\">Third person, formal and measured. Logic (logos) carries the essay; use emotion sparingly.</p>\n<h4>Writing it, step by step</h4>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Time guide for a 50-minute essay:</b> steps 1 to 5 (planning) by minute 10 · steps 6 to 8 (writing) by minute 45 · the last step (checking) in the final 5 minutes.</p>\n<ol class=\"ww-steps\">\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Unpack the issue and choose a side</h4><p>Underline the exact claim in the question. Choose the side you can <b>support best with evidence</b>, not necessarily the one you feel most strongly about. Do not sit on the fence.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>List reasons on both sides</h4><p>Draw a quick T-chart: <i>For</i> | <i>Against</i>. Pick your two strongest reasons (three for 500 words) and the strongest point on the other side.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Write the thesis</h4><p>A clear, debatable claim plus a preview of your reasons: <i>\"Secondary students should complete 40 hours of community service because it builds practical skills and strengthens communities.\"</i></p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Gather evidence for each reason</h4><p>Beside each reason, note one fact, example or expert view, and one sentence of reasoning that connects it to your claim (CER).</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Plan the counter-argument and rebuttal</h4><p>Write the opposing point in one fair sentence, then your answer to it. Outline the whole essay by minute 10.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Draft the introduction</h4><p>Hook, background, thesis.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Draft the reason paragraphs with CER</h4><p>One reason per paragraph, in thesis order. Spend most words on <b>reasoning</b>: show why the evidence proves the point.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Write the counter-argument paragraph and conclusion</h4><p>Concede fairly, rebut firmly, then close with the 3 Rs. Never introduce new evidence in the conclusion.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Check the logic, then proofread</h4><p>Remove fallacies (hasty generalisation, either–or, bandwagon, attacking the person); keep the tone respectful; fix grammar, spelling and punctuation; count your words.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Build every argument with CER</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>C</b>laim</td><td>The reason, stated as a topic sentence: \"Phones give every student instant access to research.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E</b>vidence</td><td>A fact, example or expert view that supports the claim.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>R</b>easoning</td><td>The logic that connects evidence to claim: <i>why</i> this evidence proves the reason, and why the reason supports the thesis.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>The counter-argument and rebuttal</h4>\n<p>Raising the other side, then answering it, shows the examiner that you have thought about the issue rather than just repeating your view. It also makes your position more convincing.</p>\n<ol><li><b>Concede</b> fairly: \"Admittedly, phones can distract students during lessons.\"</li>\n<li><b>Rebut</b> with logic or evidence: \"However, distraction is a problem of management, not of the device: clear rules, such as phones face-down until the teacher calls for them, keep the benefit and remove the risk.\"</li></ol>\n<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Unfair (straw man)</b>People who disagree with me just want students to fail.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Fair</b>Some teachers worry, reasonably, that phones divide attention.</div></div>\n<h4>Logical fallacies to avoid</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Hasty generalisation:</b> \"My cousin's school banned phones and grades rose, so bans always work.\"</li>\n<li><b>Either–or:</b> \"Either we ban phones or students will never learn.\"</li>\n<li><b>Bandwagon:</b> \"Everyone agrees that…\"</li>\n<li><b>Attacking the person</b> instead of the argument.</li></ul>\n<h4>Placing the counter-argument</h4>\n<p>In a 300-word essay, the most efficient plan is: introduction (45) · reason 1 (70) · reason 2 (70) · counter-argument and rebuttal (70) · conclusion (40). In 500 words you can develop three reasons before the counter-argument.</p>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"Cats Eat Rice, Crabs Run","word":"CER-CR","letters":[["C","Claim","Your reason, stated clearly."],["E","Evidence","Facts, examples, expert views."],["R","Reasoning","Why the evidence proves the claim."],["C","Counterclaim","The strongest opposing view, stated fairly."],["R","Rebuttal","Your logical answer to it."]],"extra":"Argumentative = <b>logos first</b>. Win agreement with reasons, not volume."},"check":[{"q":"Which appeal should dominate an argumentative essay?","o":["Pathos (emotion)","Logos (logic and evidence)","Humour","Kairos only"],"a":1,"why":"Argumentative writing persuades mainly through reasoning and evidence."},{"q":"What is a rebuttal?","o":["Repeating your thesis","Answering the opposing view with logic or evidence","Agreeing with the other side","A type of hook"],"a":1,"why":"After conceding the counterclaim fairly, you show why it does not defeat your position."},{"q":"\"Either we ban fast food in schools or every child will be obese.\" This is…","o":["a strong rebuttal","an either–or fallacy","a fair concession","reasoning"],"a":1,"why":"It pretends there are only two extreme options."},{"q":"Which thesis is argumentative?","o":["Fast food is sold in many Caribbean schools.","Fast food should be removed from school canteens because it harms health, lowers concentration and teaches poor habits.","Let me tell you about my lunch.","What is fast food?"],"a":1,"why":"It takes a debatable position and previews reasons."}],"task":{"prompt":"Write an argumentative essay of NO MORE than 300 words: <i>\"Students should be required to complete 40 hours of community service before they graduate from secondary school.\" Argue for OR against.</i>","min":220,"max":300,"checklist":["My thesis takes a clear side and previews my reasons.","Each reason has a claim, evidence and reasoning.","I stated the opposing view fairly.","I rebutted it with logic or evidence.","My tone is formal and I avoided fallacies.","I stayed within 300 words."]}},{"id":"persuasive","n":14,"icon":"📣","title":"The Persuasive Essay","genre":true,"kicker":"Move your audience to believe and act","photo":{"src":"img/ws/W13.jpg","w":1000,"h":665,"alt":"A smiling young woman speaking into a microphone in front of a seated audience.","by":"Dámaris Azócar","link":"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-woman-standing-in-front-of-a-crowd-holding-a-microphone-NK02ShlfWDM","cap":"A persuader speaks to a particular audience, reads the room and asks for something specific. Every technique serves one goal: moving people to act."},"objectives":["Distinguish persuasive from argumentative writing.","Combine ethos, pathos, logos and kairos for a specific audience.","Use persuasive devices and end with a clear call to action.","Follow the persuasive format and the step-by-step method to write a persuasive piece under time."],"html":"<p>A <b>persuasive essay</b> aims to change what a <b>specific audience</b> believes <i>and</i> does. Like an argumentative essay it takes a clear position, but it goes further: it uses emotion as well as logic, speaks directly to its audience and ends with a <b>call to action</b>.</p>\n<h4>Argumentative or persuasive?</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Argumentative</th><th>Persuasive</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Goal</b></td><td>Agreement through reasoning</td><td>Belief and action</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Audience</b></td><td>A general, thoughtful reader</td><td>A specific group (parents, Form 4 students, the Board)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Appeals</b></td><td>Mainly logos</td><td>Logos, ethos, pathos and kairos combined</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Voice</b></td><td>Third person, measured</td><td>May use \"we\" and \"you\" to involve the audience</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Other side</b></td><td>Counter-argument and rebuttal are essential</td><td>Objections are anticipated and answered briefly</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ending</b></td><td>A firm conclusion</td><td>A direct, specific call to action</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>The format: what goes in each paragraph</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Paragraph</th><th>What goes in it</th><th>300 words</th><th>500 words</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Attention (introduction)</b></td><td>A <b>hook</b> aimed at your audience; address them directly (\"Parents of Form 4…\"); state your position and what you want.</td><td>~45</td><td>~70</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Need</b></td><td>Show the problem and why it matters to <b>this</b> audience: facts and statistics (logos), a vivid example (pathos), your credibility (ethos).</td><td>~75</td><td>~130</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Satisfaction</b></td><td>Present your solution and how it works. Anticipate the audience's biggest objection and answer it briefly.</td><td>~75</td><td>~130</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Visualisation</b></td><td>Help them picture the result if they act, or the cost if they do not. Show why now is the time (kairos).</td><td>~55</td><td>~90</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Action (conclusion)</b></td><td>A direct, specific <b>call to action</b>: what to do, when and where. End on a memorable line (a triple or rhetorical question).</td><td>~50</td><td>~80</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"hint\">This is Monroe's Motivated Sequence set out as paragraphs. Use \"we\" and \"you\" to involve the audience, and weave in at least three AFOREST devices.</p>\n<h4>Writing it, step by step</h4>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Time guide for a 50-minute essay:</b> steps 1 to 6 (planning) by minute 10 · steps 7 and 8 (writing) by minute 45 · the last step (checking) in the final 5 minutes.</p>\n<ol class=\"ww-steps\">\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Name the audience and the action</h4><p>Complete this sentence before anything else: <i>\"I want [audience] to [action] by [when].\"</i> Example: <i>\"I want parents to sign up for phone-free family dinners this week.\"</i></p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Unpack the form and register</h4><p>Is it a speech, letter, article or flyer text? Who is reading? Set your register to suit them, always in Standard English.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Find what this audience cares about</h4><p>List two or three things that matter to them (their children's grades, money, safety, reputation). Your appeals must connect to these.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Gather one of each appeal</h4><p>Logos (a fact or statistic from a credible source), ethos (why you are worth listening to), pathos (an image that stirs feeling) and kairos (why now).</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Choose your AFOREST devices</h4><p>Pick at least three and decide where each goes: a rhetorical question in the hook, a statistic in Need, a triple in the call to action.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Plan with Monroe's five steps</h4><p>Attention · Need · Satisfaction · Visualisation · Action. Outline by minute 10.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Draft Attention, Need and Satisfaction</h4><p>Speak directly to the audience. Answer their biggest objection briefly inside Satisfaction.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Draft Visualisation and the call to action</h4><p>Paint the result, then tell them exactly what to do, when and where. A vague ending (\"we should all do better\") loses marks.</p></li>\n<li class=\"ww-step\"><h4>Check the ethics, then proofread</h4><p>Every fact must be honest; no invented statistics, fear-mongering or insults. Fix grammar, spelling and punctuation; count your words.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h4>The four appeals</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Logos:</b> facts, statistics, logical steps. \"One clean-up removes bags of plastic that would otherwise break down into the fish we eat.\"</li>\n<li><b>Ethos:</b> credibility. \"As captain of the Environmental Club, I have seen what the tide brings in every Monday.\"</li>\n<li><b>Pathos:</b> emotion. \"Imagine a turtle mistaking a plastic bag for a jellyfish.\"</li>\n<li><b>Kairos:</b> timeliness. \"Turtle-nesting season begins next month; the beach must be clear before then.\"</li></ul>\n<h4>Persuasive devices: AFOREST</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>A</b>lliteration</td><td>\"Clean coasts, clear consciences.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>F</b>acts</td><td>A specific, checkable fact from a credible source.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>O</b>pinions</td><td>Stated confidently, ideally from someone respected.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>R</b>hetorical questions</td><td>\"Do we want our children to inherit a beach of bottles?\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E</b>motive language</td><td>Words chosen for feeling: <i>choking, precious, betrayed</i>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>S</b>tatistics</td><td>Numbers that make the problem concrete (always from a credible source).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>T</b>riples (rule of three)</td><td>\"Our beach, our health and our future.\"</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>A proven structure: Monroe's Motivated Sequence</h4>\n<ol><li><b>Attention:</b> a striking hook.</li><li><b>Need:</b> show the problem and why it matters to <i>this</i> audience.</li><li><b>Satisfaction:</b> present your solution.</li><li><b>Visualisation:</b> help them picture the result if they act (or the cost if they don't).</li><li><b>Action:</b> tell them exactly what to do, when and where.</li></ol>\n<h4>Persuade ethically</h4>\n<p>Persuasion must stay honest: no invented statistics, no fear-mongering, no insulting the audience. Module 3 asks you to judge the ethics of persuasive and misleading messages, and examiners reward responsible persuasion.</p>","mnemonic":{"phrase":"AFOREST + a call to action","word":"AFOREST","letters":[["A","Alliteration","Repeated first sounds stick in the memory."],["F","Facts","Specific and checkable."],["O","Opinions","Confident, from a credible voice."],["R","Rhetorical questions","Make the audience think the answer."],["E","Emotive language","Words chosen for feeling."],["S","Statistics","Numbers that make it concrete."],["T","Triples","Ideas in threes feel complete."]],"extra":"Combine <b>logos, ethos, pathos and kairos</b>, and always end with a specific <b>call to action</b>."},"check":[{"q":"What most clearly separates persuasive from argumentative writing?","o":["Persuasive has no thesis","Persuasive targets a specific audience and ends with a call to action","Argumentative uses emotion more","They are identical"],"a":1,"why":"Persuasion aims to move a particular audience to act."},{"q":"\"Nesting season starts next month, so we must act now.\" Which appeal is this?","o":["Ethos","Pathos","Kairos","Logos only"],"a":2,"why":"It stresses the timeliness of the action."},{"q":"\"Our beach, our health and our future\" is an example of…","o":["a rhetorical question","a triple","a statistic","alliteration"],"a":1,"why":"Three parallel items: the rule of three."},{"q":"Which is the best call to action?","o":["Think about it.","Something should be done.","Meet us at the Great Bay boardwalk at 7 a.m. this Saturday with gloves and a water bottle.","Plastic is bad."],"a":2,"why":"It tells the audience exactly what to do, where, when and with what."}],"task":{"prompt":"Write a persuasive piece of NO MORE than 300 words for your school's parents, urging them to support a \"phone-free family dinner\" week. Use all four appeals and at least three AFOREST devices; label them in brackets after you finish.","min":220,"max":300,"checklist":["I wrote for a specific audience (parents).","I used logos, ethos, pathos and kairos.","I used at least three AFOREST devices.","I answered one likely objection.","I ended with a specific call to action.","I stayed within 300 words."]}},{"id":"compare","n":15,"icon":"🧩","title":"The Four Genres Side by Side","kicker":"Choose the right tools for the task","photo":null,"objectives":["Compare the four genres feature by feature.","Identify the genre a question requires.","Adapt the same topic to each genre."],"html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>📘 Expository</th><th>🔥 Narrative</th><th>⚖️ Argumentative</th><th>📣 Persuasive</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>Explain, inform</td><td>Entertain, reveal an insight</td><td>Win agreement through reasoning</td><td>Move an audience to believe and act</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Thesis</b></td><td>Previews what will be explained</td><td>Implied insight or theme</td><td>Debatable claim + reasons</td><td>Position aimed at an audience + action</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Point of view</b></td><td>Third person</td><td>First or third, consistent</td><td>Usually third</td><td>\"We\" and \"you\" allowed</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Main appeal</b></td><td>Logos (clarity)</td><td>Pathos through story</td><td>Logos + ethos</td><td>All four, including kairos</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Body</b></td><td>PEEL, one point each</td><td>Narrative arc, scenes</td><td>CER + counter-argument</td><td>Need → solution → visualise</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Evidence</b></td><td>Facts, examples, definitions</td><td>Detail, dialogue, action</td><td>Facts, logic, expert views</td><td>Facts plus emotive examples</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tone</b></td><td>Neutral, formal</td><td>Vivid, varied</td><td>Measured, formal</td><td>Passionate but controlled</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ending</b></td><td>3 Rs: significance</td><td>Resolution + insight</td><td>Firm judgement</td><td>Call to action</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mnemonic</b></td><td>Don't Panic, Cats Can Climb Ivy</td><td>Clever Cats Sit Purring Together · SWBST</td><td>Cats Eat Rice, Crabs Run</td><td>AFOREST</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h4>One topic, four genres</h4>\n<p><i>Topic: water shortages during the dry season.</i></p>\n<ul><li><b>Expository:</b> Explain three causes of water shortages on small Caribbean islands.</li>\n<li><b>Narrative:</b> Tell the story of the week the taps ran dry in your village.</li>\n<li><b>Argumentative:</b> Should every new house be required to have a rainwater cistern?</li>\n<li><b>Persuasive:</b> Convince your neighbours to cut their water use by a quarter this dry season.</li></ul>\n<h4>Spot the genre in the question</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>If the question says…</th><th>The genre is…</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>explain, describe how, outline the causes, discuss the effects, account for</td><td>Expository</td></tr>\n<tr><td>write a story, tell of a time, begin with the words…</td><td>Narrative</td></tr>\n<tr><td>argue for or against, to what extent do you agree, take a position</td><td>Argumentative</td></tr>\n<tr><td>convince, urge, persuade [a named audience] to…</td><td>Persuasive</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>","mnemonic":null,"check":[{"q":"\"Convince the Parent-Teacher Association to fund a school counsellor.\" Genre?","o":["Expository","Narrative","Argumentative","Persuasive"],"a":3,"why":"\"Convince\" plus a named audience and an implied action signals persuasive writing."},{"q":"\"Describe how a rumour spreads through a small community.\" Genre?","o":["Expository (process)","Narrative","Argumentative","Persuasive"],"a":0,"why":"\"Describe how\" asks you to explain a process."},{"q":"Which genre most needs a counter-argument and rebuttal?","o":["Narrative","Expository","Argumentative","None"],"a":2,"why":"Answering the other side fairly is essential to reasoned argument."},{"q":"Which genre usually uses an implied thesis?","o":["Narrative","Expository","Argumentative","Persuasive"],"a":0,"why":"A story reveals its insight through events rather than announcing it."}],"task":{"prompt":"Choose a topic you care about. Write a one-sentence thesis (or SWBST line for the narrative) for EACH of the four genres on that topic, and name the main appeal each would use.","min":60,"max":180,"checklist":["I wrote four genre-appropriate theses.","The expository thesis takes no side.","The persuasive thesis names an audience or action.","I named the main appeal for each."]}}],"builds":[{"id":"b-exp","genre":"expository","icon":"📘","name":"Expository","method":"Cause and effect","question":"In an essay of NO MORE than 300 words, explain THREE causes of traffic congestion in a Caribbean capital of your choice.","steps":[{"label":"Unpack the question (BUG)","html":"<p><b>Box</b> the command word: <span class=\"ww-box\">explain</span> → give reasons and show <i>how</i> each cause works.<br><b>Underline</b> key terms: <u>THREE</u> · <u>causes</u> (not effects, not solutions) · <u>traffic congestion</u> · <u>a Caribbean capital</u>.<br>Limits: 300 words · expository · I choose the capital: <b>Philipsburg</b>, which I know well.</p><p><b>Method of exposition:</b> cause and effect.</p>"},{"label":"Prewrite: brainstorm, then cluster","html":"<p>Two-minute brainstorm (no judging): <i>narrow streets · one-way system · cruise ships · taxis and tour buses · more cars per family · minibuses with no timetable · school drop-off times · road works · Causeway helps? · delivery trucks unloading · tourists walking in the road · few parking spaces · rain floods roads</i></p><p>Grouped into three branches:</p><div class=\"ww-cluster\"><div class=\"c0\">Causes of congestion</div><div class=\"cb\"><b>Layout</b><span>narrow historic streets</span><span>trucks unloading block lanes</span></div><div class=\"cb\"><b>More cars</b><span>families own two or more</span><span>minibuses not scheduled</span></div><div class=\"cb\"><b>Cruise days</b><span>thousands of visitors</span><span>taxis, tour buses, shuttles</span></div></div><p class=\"hint\">Dropped: \"Causeway helps\" (a solution, not a cause), \"rain floods roads\" (occasional, weaker), \"school drop-off\" (fits inside \"more cars\").</p>"},{"label":"Research and evidence","html":"<p>No internet in the exam, so the evidence is specific <b>local knowledge and examples</b>:</p><ul><li>Front Street and Back Street: narrow, built-up on both sides.</li><li>Public transport is mainly privately run minibuses without fixed timetables.</li><li>Large cruise ships bring thousands of visitors into the harbour in one morning.</li></ul><p class=\"hint\">For a class essay you could strengthen this with figures from the national statistics department or the port authority, checked with CRAAP.</p>"},{"label":"Write the thesis (TAP)","html":"<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">First try</b>Traffic in Philipsburg is very bad for many reasons.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">TAP thesis</b>Traffic congestion in Philipsburg is caused mainly by its narrow historic streets, rising private car ownership and the crowds that arrive on cruise-ship days.</div></div><p><b>T</b> traffic congestion in Philipsburg · <b>A</b> caused mainly by three factors · <b>P</b> streets, car ownership, cruise days. SOS: specific ✓ one sentence ✓ supportable ✓</p>"},{"label":"Outline with a word budget","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p>Intro (≈55): hook (slow drive) → bridge (squeezed capital) → thesis<br>¶1 Layout (≈65): narrow streets → truck unloading → no alternative routes → bottleneck<br>¶2 More cars (≈65): minibuses unscheduled → families choose cars → same peak hours<br>¶3 Cruise days (≈55): thousands of visitors → taxis, buses → same hours as locals<br>Conclusion (≈45): 3 Rs</p></div>"},{"label":"Choose a hook","html":"<ul><li><b>Question:</b> Why does a five-minute trip take half an hour?</li><li><b>Unusual fact:</b> Saint Martin is one small island shared by two countries…</li><li><b>Scene:</b> <i>On a busy weekday afternoon, the short drive from Philipsburg to Cole Bay can take many times longer than it should.</i> ✓</li></ul><p class=\"hint\">Chosen: the scene, because every reader on the island recognises it and it leads straight to the topic.</p>"},{"label":"Draft the introduction (HBT)","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag h\">Hook</span> On a busy weekday afternoon, the short drive from Philipsburg to Cole Bay can take many times longer than it should. <span class=\"ww-tag b\">Bridge</span> For a capital squeezed between the Great Salt Pond and the sea, every extra vehicle competes for the same few streets. <span class=\"ww-tag t\">Thesis</span> Traffic congestion in Philipsburg is caused mainly by its narrow historic streets, rising private car ownership and the crowds that arrive on cruise-ship days.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft the body (PEEL)","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag p\">P</span> First, Philipsburg's narrow historic streets were laid out for carts and pedestrians, not for thousands of cars. <span class=\"ww-tag e\">E</span> Front Street and Back Street are squeezed between rows of buildings, with almost no room to widen them. <span class=\"ww-tag x\">E</span> When a single delivery truck stops to unload, the lane stops behind it, and because there are few alternative routes, the delay spreads quickly. <span class=\"ww-tag l\">L</span> The town's layout therefore creates a bottleneck before a single extra car arrives.</p><p><span class=\"ww-tag p\">P</span> Second, private car ownership has grown faster than the road network. <span class=\"ww-tag e\">E</span> Public transport is mostly provided by privately run minibuses without fixed timetables, so many families prefer the reliability of their own cars, and some own two or more. <span class=\"ww-tag x\">E</span> As a result, more vehicles enter the same limited roads every morning and leave together every afternoon, <span class=\"ww-tag l\">L</span> filling them at predictable peak times.</p><p><span class=\"ww-tag p\">P</span> Finally, cruise-ship days add a sudden surge. <span class=\"ww-tag e\">E</span> Thousands of visitors can arrive at the harbour in a single morning, and they need taxis, tour buses and shuttles. <span class=\"ww-tag x\">E</span> All of these vehicles converge on the roads around the harbour and the town centre <span class=\"ww-tag l\">L</span> at the same hours as local traffic.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft the conclusion (3 Rs)","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag t\">Restate</span> Philipsburg's traffic is not simply bad luck. <span class=\"ww-tag b\">Recap</span> Old streets, more cars and cruise-day crowds combine to fill a small capital beyond its capacity. <span class=\"ww-tag h\">Resonate</span> Understanding these causes is the first step towards a capital where a short drive takes a short time.</p></div>"},{"label":"Revise (ARMS)","html":"<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Draft sentence</b>Also I think the cruise ships are the worst cause because tourists are everywhere and it is really annoying.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Revised</b>Finally, cruise-ship days add a sudden surge.</div></div><p><b>R</b>emoved opinion (\"I think\", \"worst\", \"annoying\"): expository writing is objective. <b>S</b>ubstituted a precise claim. <b>M</b>oved cruise days to the last body paragraph to match the thesis order.</p>"},{"label":"Edit (CUPS) and count","html":"<ul><li><b>C</b>: front street → Front Street; philipsburg → Philipsburg.</li><li><b>U</b>: \"families prefers\" → \"families prefer\".</li><li><b>P</b>: \"the lane stops behind it, there are few routes\" (comma splice) → \"…, and because there are few alternative routes,…\".</li><li><b>S</b>: \"pedestrains\" → \"pedestrians\"; \"alternitive\" → \"alternative\".</li></ul><p>Word count checked: under 300.</p>"}],"title":"What Jams Philipsburg's Streets?","final":["On a busy weekday afternoon, the short drive from Philipsburg to Cole Bay can take many times longer than it should. For a capital squeezed between the Great Salt Pond and the sea, every extra vehicle competes for the same few streets. Traffic congestion in Philipsburg is caused mainly by its narrow historic streets, rising private car ownership and the crowds that arrive on cruise-ship days.","First, Philipsburg's narrow historic streets were laid out for carts and pedestrians, not for thousands of cars. Front Street and Back Street are squeezed between rows of buildings, with almost no room to widen them. When a single delivery truck stops to unload, the lane stops behind it, and because there are few alternative routes, the delay spreads quickly. The town's layout therefore creates a bottleneck before a single extra car arrives.","Second, private car ownership has grown faster than the road network. Public transport is mostly provided by privately run minibuses without fixed timetables, so many families prefer the reliability of their own cars, and some own two or more. As a result, more vehicles enter the same limited roads every morning and leave together every afternoon, filling them at predictable peak times.","Finally, cruise-ship days add a sudden surge. Thousands of visitors can arrive at the harbour in a single morning, and they need taxis, tour buses and shuttles. All of these vehicles converge on the roads around the harbour and the town centre at the same hours as local traffic.","Philipsburg's traffic is not simply bad luck. Old streets, more cars and cruise-day crowds combine to fill a small capital beyond its capacity. Understanding these causes is the first step towards a capital where a short drive takes a short time."],"marks":[["Content",9,10,"Three distinct causes, each explained with how it produces congestion and a specific local example. A statistic would lift it to 10."],["Organization",7,7,"HBT introduction, thesis order followed exactly, ACCESS transitions, 3R conclusion."],["Expression",7,8,"Objective third person, precise vocabulary, varied sentences, accurate Standard English."]]},{"id":"b-nar","genre":"narrative","icon":"🔥","name":"Narrative","method":"Narrative arc","question":"Write a story of NO MORE than 300 words which begins: \"The radio crackled just as the lights went out.\"","steps":[{"label":"Unpack the question (BUG)","html":"<p><b>Box:</b> <span class=\"ww-box\">write a story</span> → narrative genre.<br><b>Underline:</b> must <u>begin</u> with the exact sentence given · <u>300 words</u>.<br><b>Glance back:</b> the opening line suggests a storm or blackout, but any situation that fits it is allowed.</p>"},{"label":"Prewrite: 5Ws and H, then freewrite","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody><tr><td>Who?</td><td>Kezia (12), her little brothers, Granny</td></tr><tr><td>What?</td><td>A hurricane night; power and radio fail</td></tr><tr><td>When?</td><td>Night into dawn</td></tr><tr><td>Where?</td><td>The windowless hallway of Granny's house</td></tr><tr><td>Why does it matter?</td><td>Kezia wants to be brave for her brothers but cannot</td></tr><tr><td>How is it resolved?</td><td>Granny tells Anancy stories until dawn</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"hint\">A three-minute freewrite produced the best detail: Granny saying \"Not tonight\" to the roof.</p>"},{"label":"Gather authentic detail","html":"<p>Research for a narrative is <b>sensory and cultural detail</b> that makes the setting real: galvanize roof, a mattress dragged into the hallway, candle and matches, Anancy stories, a mango tree down in the yard, water pooling in the kitchen. Older relatives often remember Hurricane Donna (1960) and Hurricane Luis (1995).</p>"},{"label":"Plot with SWBST and find the insight","html":"<p><b>S</b> Kezia <b>W</b> wanted to keep her brothers calm <b>B</b> but the power and radio failed and she was too frightened to speak, <b>S</b> so Granny lit a candle and told stories all night, <b>T</b> then Kezia realised that calm can shelter people as surely as a roof.</p><p><b>Controlling insight (implied thesis):</b> a calm voice can be stronger shelter than any roof.</p>"},{"label":"Map the narrative arc","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><b>Exposition</b> (≈45): radio dies; hallway; brothers.<br><b>Rising action</b> (≈60): static; roof slam; Kofi cries; Kezia cannot speak.<br><b>Climax</b> (≈70): Granny lights the match and begins a story.<br><b>Falling action</b> (≈60): hours of stories; \"Not tonight\"; brothers sleep.<br><b>Resolution</b> (≈50): dawn, damage, safety, insight.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft the opening (hook is given)","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p>The radio crackled just as the lights went out.</p><p>Outside, the wind had stopped whistling and started roaring. My little brothers, Jaden and Kofi, pressed against me on the mattress we had dragged into the hallway, the only part of Granny's house without a window.</p></div><p class=\"hint\">The given line is the hook. The second paragraph sets the scene (who, where) through action and sound rather than explanation.</p>"},{"label":"Draft the rising action and climax","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p>\"Kezia, the radio dead?\" Jaden whispered.</p><p>I twisted the dial. Static, then silence. Something heavy slammed against the galvanize roof, and Kofi began to cry. I wanted to tell them everything would be fine, but my voice had disappeared somewhere in my chest.</p><p>Then a match flared. Granny lowered herself onto the floor, set a candle between us and folded her hands as if we were on her verandah on a Sunday evening.</p></div><p class=\"hint\">Show, don't tell: the reader sees Kezia's fear (\"my voice had disappeared\") instead of being told \"I was scared\". Creole appears only in dialogue.</p>"},{"label":"Draft the falling action","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p>\"This is not my first storm,\" she said. \"When Hurricane Donna came, my mother told us Anancy stories until morning. Who want to hear how Anancy tried to trap the wind in a calabash?\"</p><p>Kofi sniffed. \"He catch it?\"</p><p>\"That depends on whether you let me finish.\"</p><p>Hour after hour, Granny's voice rose and fell with the wind. Each time the roof groaned, she looked up and said, \"Not tonight,\" as if she were speaking to the storm itself. Somewhere in the third story, Jaden's head grew heavy on my shoulder.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft the resolution and insight","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p>By dawn the wind had dropped to a sulk. Water had pooled in the kitchen and the mango tree lay across the yard, but we were safe.</p><p>The roof leaked until morning, yet I have never felt more sheltered than I did inside the sound of Granny's voice.</p></div><p class=\"hint\">The insight is revealed in a final image, not as a lecture (\"The moral of this story is…\").</p>"},{"label":"Revise (ARMS)","html":"<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Draft</b>Then we went to sleep and then in the morning we woke up and then we saw the damage and then we were happy.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Revised</b>By dawn the wind had dropped to a sulk. Water had pooled in the kitchen and the mango tree lay across the yard, but we were safe.</div></div><p><b>R</b>emoved the \"and then\" chain. <b>S</b>ubstituted specific images for \"the damage\" and \"happy\". <b>A</b>dded personification (\"dropped to a sulk\").</p>"},{"label":"Edit (CUPS) and count","html":"<ul><li><b>C</b>: hurricane donna → Hurricane Donna; anancy → Anancy.</li><li><b>U</b>: tense shift \"the wind stops\" → \"the wind had stopped\" (narration stays in the past).</li><li><b>P</b>: question mark inside the closing quotation mark; a new line for each speaker.</li><li><b>S</b>: \"verandah\", \"calabash\" checked.</li></ul><p class=\"hint\">Creole forms in dialogue (\"the radio dead?\", \"He catch it?\") are deliberate and stay.</p>"}],"title":"Not Tonight","final":["The radio crackled just as the lights went out.","Outside, the wind had stopped whistling and started roaring. My little brothers, Jaden and Kofi, pressed against me on the mattress we had dragged into the hallway, the only part of Granny's house without a window.","\"Kezia, the radio dead?\" Jaden whispered.","I twisted the dial. Static, then silence. Something heavy slammed against the galvanize roof, and Kofi began to cry. I wanted to tell them everything would be fine, but my voice had disappeared somewhere in my chest.","Then a match flared. Granny lowered herself onto the floor, set a candle between us and folded her hands as if we were on her verandah on a Sunday evening.","\"This is not my first storm,\" she said. \"When Hurricane Donna came, my mother told us Anancy stories until morning. Who want to hear how Anancy tried to trap the wind in a calabash?\"","Kofi sniffed. \"He catch it?\"","\"That depends on whether you let me finish.\"","Hour after hour, Granny's voice rose and fell with the wind. Each time the roof groaned, she looked up and said, \"Not tonight,\" as if she were speaking to the storm itself. Somewhere in the third story, Jaden's head grew heavy on my shoulder.","By dawn the wind had dropped to a sulk. Water had pooled in the kitchen and the mango tree lay across the yard, but we were safe.","The roof leaked until morning, yet I have never felt more sheltered than I did inside the sound of Granny's voice."],"marks":[["Content",9,10,"Clear conflict, a turning point and a resolution; vivid sensory detail and an insight revealed through image."],["Organization",7,7,"Complete arc, correct paragraphing for speakers, time transitions (Then, Hour after hour, By dawn)."],["Expression",7,8,"Consistent past tense, correctly punctuated dialogue, Creole confined to speech, varied sentences."]]},{"id":"b-arg","genre":"argumentative","icon":"⚖️","name":"Argumentative","method":"Claim, evidence, reasoning + rebuttal","question":"In an essay of NO MORE than 300 words, argue for OR against the view that secondary schools should allow students to use mobile phones in class for learning.","steps":[{"label":"Unpack the question (BUG)","html":"<p><b>Box:</b> <span class=\"ww-box\">argue for OR against</span> → argumentative; pick ONE side.<br><b>Underline:</b> <u>secondary schools</u> · <u>in class</u> · <u>for learning</u> (not for social use) · <u>300 words</u>.<br><b>Glance back:</b> the phrase \"for learning\" lets me argue for <i>guided</i> use, which is easier to defend.</p>"},{"label":"Prewrite: T-chart","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><thead><tr><th>For</th><th>Against</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>instant research</td><td>distraction, messaging</td></tr><tr><td>builds digital responsibility</td><td>cyberbullying</td></tr><tr><td>free to the school</td><td>not every student has a phone</td></tr><tr><td>calculators, dictionaries, cameras</td><td>cheating in tests</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"hint\">Decision: argue <b>for</b>. Strongest reasons: research access and digital responsibility. Strongest objection to rebut: distraction.</p>"},{"label":"Research and evidence","html":"<p>Evidence I can use without statistics: what the course itself asks students to do (Module 1 tests searching for and evaluating sources); what workplaces and universities expect. For a class essay, look for a ministry of education policy or a study on phones in classrooms and CRAAP-test it. Be careful: research on phones in class is <b>mixed</b>, so the honest claim is about <i>guided</i> use.</p>"},{"label":"Write the thesis (TAP)","html":"<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">On the fence</b>Phones in class have good and bad sides.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">TAP thesis</b>Secondary schools should allow mobile phones in class for guided learning, because they provide instant access to information and build the digital responsibility students will need after school.</div></div>"},{"label":"Outline with a word budget","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p>Intro (≈55): hook (pocket library) → bridge (treated as distraction) → thesis<br>Reason 1 (≈65): research access → Communication Studies example → Module 1 skills<br>Reason 2 (≈60): responsibility → work and university → prepared, not shielded<br>Counter + rebuttal (≈60): distraction → management, rules<br>Conclusion (≈45): 3 Rs, firm judgement</p></div>"},{"label":"Choose a hook","html":"<ul><li><b>Bold statement:</b> <i>Most Caribbean secondary students carry a more powerful research tool in their pockets than many school libraries can offer.</i> ✓</li><li><b>Question:</b> Why do we ban the one device every student already owns?</li></ul><p class=\"hint\">The bold statement is stronger: it plants my first reason before the thesis.</p>"},{"label":"Draft the introduction","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag h\">Hook</span> Most Caribbean secondary students carry a more powerful research tool in their pockets than many school libraries can offer. <span class=\"ww-tag b\">Bridge</span> Yet many schools still treat the phone only as a distraction. <span class=\"ww-tag t\">Thesis</span> Secondary schools should allow mobile phones in class for guided learning, because they provide instant access to information and build the digital responsibility students will need after school.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft the reasons (CER)","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag p\">Claim</span> First, phones give every student immediate access to up-to-date information. <span class=\"ww-tag e\">Evidence</span> In a Communication Studies lesson, a student can check the date of a source, compare two news reports or find a definition in seconds. <span class=\"ww-tag x\">Reasoning</span> This turns the lesson from listening into investigating, and searching and evaluating sources are exactly the skills Module 1 assesses.</p><p><span class=\"ww-tag p\">Claim</span> Furthermore, guided use teaches responsibility rather than avoidance. <span class=\"ww-tag e\">Evidence</span> Students will use phones at work and at university with no teacher to confiscate them. <span class=\"ww-tag x\">Reasoning</span> If school is where they learn when to search, when to switch off and how to judge what they read, they leave prepared for the digital world rather than shielded from it.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft the counter-argument and rebuttal","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag b\">Concede</span> Admittedly, phones can distract students, and some will try to message friends during lessons. <span class=\"ww-tag l\">Rebut</span> However, distraction is a problem of classroom management, not of the device. Clear rules, such as phones face-down until the teacher calls for them, keep the benefits while limiting the risk, just as schools manage other tools that can be misused.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft the conclusion (3 Rs)","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag t\">Restate</span> Banning phones removes a problem only by removing an opportunity. <span class=\"ww-tag b\">Recap</span> With clear rules, phones widen access to knowledge and teach lifelong digital habits. <span class=\"ww-tag h\">Resonate</span> Used wisely, the phone in a student's pocket is not a distraction but the most powerful textbook the school will never have to buy.</p></div>"},{"label":"Revise (ARMS) and edit (CUPS)","html":"<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Draft</b>People who want to ban phones are just old-fashioned and don't understand young people!!</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Revised</b>Admittedly, phones can distract students, and some will try to message friends during lessons.</div></div><p><b>S</b>ubstituted a fair concession for an attack on the person (a fallacy). CUPS: removed double exclamation marks; \"it's benefits\" → \"its benefits\"; checked word count.</p>"}],"title":"Phones in Class: A Tool Worth Using","final":["Most Caribbean secondary students carry a more powerful research tool in their pockets than many school libraries can offer. Yet many schools still treat the phone only as a distraction. Secondary schools should allow mobile phones in class for guided learning, because they provide instant access to information and build the digital responsibility students will need after school.","First, phones give every student immediate access to up-to-date information. In a Communication Studies lesson, a student can check the date of a source, compare two news reports or find a definition in seconds. This turns the lesson from listening into investigating, and searching and evaluating sources are exactly the skills Module 1 assesses.","Furthermore, guided use teaches responsibility rather than avoidance. Students will use phones at work and at university with no teacher to confiscate them. If school is where they learn when to search, when to switch off and how to judge what they read, they leave prepared for the digital world rather than shielded from it.","Admittedly, phones can distract students, and some will try to message friends during lessons. However, distraction is a problem of classroom management, not of the device. Clear rules, such as phones face-down until the teacher calls for them, keep the benefits while limiting the risk, just as schools manage other tools that can be misused.","Banning phones removes a problem only by removing an opportunity. With clear rules, phones widen access to knowledge and teach lifelong digital habits. Used wisely, the phone in a student's pocket is not a distraction but the most powerful textbook the school will never have to buy."],"marks":[["Content",9,10,"Two well-reasoned arguments and a fair, answered counter-argument. One concrete piece of research evidence would earn 10."],["Organization",7,7,"Clear thesis, CER paragraphs, counter-argument placed before the conclusion, strong transitions."],["Expression",7,8,"Measured, formal tone; no fallacies; precise vocabulary; accurate Standard English."]]},{"id":"b-per","genre":"persuasive","icon":"📣","name":"Persuasive","method":"Monroe's Motivated Sequence","question":"Write a persuasive piece of NO MORE than 300 words for your school community, urging students to join a beach clean-up this Saturday.","steps":[{"label":"Unpack the question (BUG)","html":"<p><b>Box:</b> <span class=\"ww-box\">urging</span> → persuasive; must move the audience to act.<br><b>Underline:</b> <u>school community</u> (students: use \"we\" and \"you\") · <u>beach clean-up</u> · <u>this Saturday</u> (a built-in kairos) · <u>300 words</u>.</p>"},{"label":"Prewrite: profile the audience","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"gn-tbl\"><tbody><tr><td>Who are they?</td><td>Students aged 12–18, busy, sociable, care about friends and the beach</td></tr><tr><td>What do they already believe?</td><td>Litter is bad, but \"someone else will clean it\"</td></tr><tr><td>Likely objection</td><td>\"One morning won't make a difference.\"</td></tr><tr><td>What will move them?</td><td>Friends going together, a visible result, the beach they use</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"label":"Research and evidence","html":"<p>Logos I can state honestly: plastic does not biodegrade; sunlight and waves break it into small pieces that marine life swallows; tourism supports many island families. Ethos: I am captain of the Environmental Club and have done clean-ups. I will <b>not</b> invent statistics; a simple, honest multiplication (100 students × 1 bag) is safer and still powerful.</p>"},{"label":"Write the thesis","html":"<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Weak</b>Beach clean-ups are good and people should go.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Persuasive thesis</b>Every student at our school should give one Saturday morning to the Great Bay clean-up, because our beach, our health and our future depend on it.</div></div><p class=\"hint\">The thesis names the audience (every student), the action (one Saturday morning) and a triple (beach, health, future).</p>"},{"label":"Outline with Monroe's sequence","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><b>Attention</b> (≈55): bold-statement hook → Monday litter → thesis<br><b>Need</b> (≈60): ethos (club captain) + logos (plastic → fish → plates)<br><b>Satisfaction + Visualisation</b> (≈60): pathos (turtle) + picture the clean beach + kairos<br><b>Objection</b> (≈45): \"one morning won't matter\" → 100 bags<br><b>Action</b> (≈40): who, where, when, what to bring</p></div>"},{"label":"Choose a hook","html":"<ul><li><b>Truthful bold statement:</b> <i>The most dangerous thing on Great Bay beach is not the sea; it is what we leave behind.</i> ✓</li><li><b>Rhetorical question:</b> Would you swim in a rubbish bin?</li></ul><p class=\"hint\">The bold statement surprises without exaggerating, which keeps ethos intact.</p>"},{"label":"Draft attention and need","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p>The most dangerous thing on Great Bay beach is not the sea; it is what we leave behind. Every Monday, the tide returns bottles, straws and food containers to the sand where we swim. Every student at our school should give one Saturday morning to the Great Bay clean-up, because our beach, our health and our future depend on it.</p><p><span class=\"ww-tag e\">Ethos</span> As captain of the Environmental Club, I have walked that shoreline after a busy weekend and filled a bag in less than ten minutes. <span class=\"ww-tag p\">Logos</span> Plastic does not disappear; sunlight and waves break it into tiny pieces that fish swallow, and those fish end up on our plates. Pollution on the beach soon becomes pollution in our food.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft visualisation and objection","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p><span class=\"ww-tag x\">Pathos</span> Imagine a turtle mistaking a floating bag for a jellyfish. Now imagine the same beach clean, the sand bright and the water clear for the visitors whose spending supports so many of our families. <span class=\"ww-tag l\">Kairos</span> This Saturday is our last clean-up before the holiday season brings the busiest weeks of the year.</p><p><span class=\"ww-tag b\">Objection</span> Some of you will say one morning cannot make a difference. Yet if one hundred students each fill one bag, that is one hundred bags of rubbish that will never reach the sea. Small actions, multiplied, become real change.</p></div>"},{"label":"Draft the call to action","html":"<div class=\"passage\"><p>Great Bay has given us swims, picnics and memories. Now it needs something back. Bring a hat, bring a friend and meet us at the boardwalk at seven o'clock on Saturday morning. Clean coasts start with clear choices.</p></div><p class=\"hint\">AFOREST check: alliteration (Clean coasts… clear choices), facts, opinion, rhetorical device (Imagine…), emotive language (dangerous), statistics (one hundred bags), triples (beach, health, future).</p>"},{"label":"Revise (ARMS) and edit (CUPS)","html":"<div class=\"ww-fix\"><div class=\"weak\"><b class=\"lab\">Draft</b>Millions of turtles die EVERY DAY because of us!!! Come or you don't care about the planet.</div><div class=\"strong\"><b class=\"lab\">Revised</b>Imagine a turtle mistaking a floating bag for a jellyfish.</div></div><p><b>R</b>emoved an invented statistic and a guilt-trip insult (unethical persuasion). <b>S</b>ubstituted a vivid, honest image. CUPS: \"great bay\" → \"Great Bay\"; \"were\" → \"where\"; word count checked.</p>"}],"title":"One Saturday for Great Bay","final":["The most dangerous thing on Great Bay beach is not the sea; it is what we leave behind. Every Monday, the tide returns bottles, straws and food containers to the sand where we swim. Every student at our school should give one Saturday morning to the Great Bay clean-up, because our beach, our health and our future depend on it.","As captain of the Environmental Club, I have walked that shoreline after a busy weekend and filled a bag in less than ten minutes. Plastic does not disappear; sunlight and waves break it into tiny pieces that fish swallow, and those fish end up on our plates. Pollution on the beach soon becomes pollution in our food.","Imagine a turtle mistaking a floating bag for a jellyfish. Now imagine the same beach clean, the sand bright and the water clear for the visitors whose spending supports so many of our families. This Saturday is our last clean-up before the holiday season brings the busiest weeks of the year.","Some of you will say one morning cannot make a difference. Yet if one hundred students each fill one bag, that is one hundred bags of rubbish that will never reach the sea. Small actions, multiplied, become real change.","Great Bay has given us swims, picnics and memories. Now it needs something back. Bring a hat, bring a friend and meet us at the boardwalk at seven o'clock on Saturday morning. Clean coasts start with clear choices."],"marks":[["Content",9,10,"All four appeals used purposefully, an objection answered and a specific call to action."],["Organization",7,7,"Monroe's sequence from attention to action; each paragraph has one job."],["Expression",8,8,"Direct, warm register for a student audience; AFOREST devices used with control; accurate Standard English."]]}],"title":"Essay Writing Workshop","intro":"<p>Every essay you write in Communication Studies is built the same way, whatever its genre: you <b>unpack the question</b>, <b>generate and research ideas</b>, commit to a <b>thesis</b>, <b>hook</b> your reader, develop <b>focused body paragraphs</b>, connect them with <b>transitions</b>, close with purpose, then <b>revise and edit</b>. This workshop teaches each stage step by step, then shows you how the four genres you must master (<b>expository, narrative, argumentative and persuasive</b>) use those stages differently.</p>\n<p>Each chapter has notes, a photo, a memory aid (mnemonic) you can flip, a quick check and a short writing task. The <b>Build-a-Sample</b> section then constructs one Grade One essay in each genre from the blank page to the final draft, so you can watch every decision being made.</p>","specs":{"note":"Paper 02 lasts 2 hours 30 minutes and has three compulsory essays, one per module: about 50 minutes each, including 10 minutes to plan and 5 to proofread.","rows":[["Paper 02 · Module 1","Expository essay analysing an extract: main message, writer's purpose, strategies or techniques, credibility","No more than 500 words","25 marks: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8"],["Paper 02 · Module 2","Expository essay on language in a transcript or extract: features of Creole and Standard English, attitudes, identity, technology","No more than 500 words","25 marks: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8"],["Paper 02 · Module 3","Expository response to a communication scenario, or a genre essay (expository, narrative, argumentative or persuasive) in class practice","No more than 300 words","25 marks: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8"],["Paper 032 · Section B","Reflective or expository piece on the year's topic for a stated audience, plus a justification","3(a) 200–250 words · 3(b) 50–100 words","40 marks: 3(a) 25, 3(b) 15"]],"scheme":[["Content",10,"You answer every part of the question accurately, with relevant, developed points and specific evidence or examples."],["Organization",7,"Introduction with a clear thesis, one point per paragraph, logical order, smooth transitions, a conclusion that closes the argument."],["Expression",8,"Standard English throughout: accurate grammar, spelling and punctuation, varied sentences, precise vocabulary and a register that fits the audience."]]}};
